(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqSets=[{pn:1,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B(\u03B1, \u03B2) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D : |\u03B1 \u2212 1| \u2264 4 and |\u03B2 \u2212 5| \u2264 6\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B(\u03B1, \u03B2) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D : 16(\u03B1 \u2212 2)\xB2 + 9(\u03B2 \u2212 6)\xB2 \u2264 144\uFF5D. Then",o:["A \u2282 B","neither A \u2282 B nor B \u2282 A","B \u2282 A","A \u222A B = \uFF5B(x, y) : \u22124 \u2264 x \u2264 4, \u22121 \u2264 y \u2264 11\uFF5D"],c:2,s:`Step 1: |\u03B1 \u2212 1| \u2264 4 means \u22123 \u2264 \u03B1 \u2264 5, and |\u03B2 \u2212 5| \u2264 6 means \u22121 \u2264 \u03B2 \u2264 11. So A is a rectangle with \u03B1 from \u22123 to 5 and \u03B2 from \u22121 to 11.
Step 2: Divide 16(\u03B1 \u2212 2)\xB2 + 9(\u03B2 \u2212 6)\xB2 \u2264 144 by 144: (\u03B1 \u2212 2)\xB2/9 + (\u03B2 \u2212 6)\xB2/16 \u2264 1. This is the inside of an ellipse with centre (2, 6).
Step 3: The semi-axes are 3 along \u03B1 and 4 along \u03B2. So \u03B1 goes from \u22121 to 5 and \u03B2 goes from 2 to 10 for the ellipse.
Step 4: The ellipse fits inside the box \u22121 \u2264 \u03B1 \u2264 5, 2 \u2264 \u03B2 \u2264 10, and that box lies inside the rectangle A (\u22123 to 5 and \u22121 to 11). So every point of B is in A.
Step 5: A has points such as (\u22123, 5) that are not in B, so A is not inside B. Also option (d) is wrong because A already reaches \u03B1 = \u22123 and \u03B1 = 5, outside \u22124 \u2264 x \u2264 4 on the right.
Answer: B \u2282 A, option (c).`},{pn:2,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, \u2026, 10\uFF5D and B = \uFF5Bm/n : m, n \u2208 A, m < n and gcd(m, n) = 1\uFF5D. Then n(B) is equal to :",o:["36","31","37","29"],c:1,s:`Step 1: B has fractions m/n with m < n and no common factor. Fractions in lowest terms are all different numbers, so we just count the pairs (m, n).
Step 2: For a fixed n, the number of m with 1 \u2264 m < n and gcd(m, n) = 1 is \u03C6(n) (Euler totient).
Step 3: n = 2: 1, n = 3: 2, n = 4: 2, n = 5: 4, n = 6: 2, n = 7: 6, n = 8: 4, n = 9: 6, n = 10: 4.
Step 4: Add them: 1 + 2 + 2 + 4 + 2 + 6 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 31 (also checked by a short program).
Answer: 31, option (b).`},{pn:3,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"Let A = \uFF5Bn \u2208 [100, 700] \u2229 \u2115 : n is neither a multiple of 3 nor a multiple of 4\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in A is",o:["280","300","310","290"],c:1,s:`Step 1: The numbers from 100 to 700 are 601 in all.
Step 2: Multiples of 3: first 102, last 699, so (699 \u2212 102)/3 + 1 = 200.
Step 3: Multiples of 4: first 100, last 700, so (700 \u2212 100)/4 + 1 = 151.
Step 4: Multiples of both 3 and 4 are multiples of 12: first 108, last 696, so (696 \u2212 108)/12 + 1 = 50.
Step 5: Multiples of 3 or 4 = 200 + 151 \u2212 50 = 301 (inclusion-exclusion).
Step 6: Neither = 601 \u2212 301 = 300.
Answer: 300, option (b).`},{pn:4,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let S = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : (\u221A3 + \u221A2)\u02E3 + (\u221A3 \u2212 \u221A2)\u02E3 = 10\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in S is",o:["4","2","0","1"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Note (\u221A3 + \u221A2)(\u221A3 \u2212 \u221A2) = 3 \u2212 2 = 1, so (\u221A3 \u2212 \u221A2)\u02E3 = 1/(\u221A3 + \u221A2)\u02E3.
Step 2: Put t = (\u221A3 + \u221A2)\u02E3 (t > 0). The equation becomes t + 1/t = 10, i.e. t\xB2 \u2212 10t + 1 = 0.
Step 3: t = (10 \xB1 \u221A96)/2 = 5 \xB1 2\u221A6. Both are positive.
Step 4: (\u221A3 + \u221A2)\xB2 = 5 + 2\u221A6 and (\u221A3 + \u221A2)\u207B\xB2 = 5 \u2212 2\u221A6. So t = (\u221A3 + \u221A2)\u02E3 gives x = 2 or x = \u22122.
Step 5: Each positive t gives exactly one x, so S = \uFF5B2, \u22122\uFF5D.
Answer: 2 elements, option (b).`},{pn:5,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let A and B be two finite sets with m and n elements respectively. The total number of subsets of the set A is 56 more than the total number of subsets of B. Then the distance of the point P(m, n) from the point Q(\u22122, \u22123) is",o:["6","8","10","4"],c:2,s:`Step 1: A set with m elements has 2\u1D50 subsets, so 2\u1D50 \u2212 2\u207F = 56.
Step 2: Factor: 2\u207F(2^(m\u2212n) \u2212 1) = 56 = 8 \xD7 7. The odd part 2^(m\u2212n) \u2212 1 must be 7 and 2\u207F = 8.
Step 3: So n = 3 and 2^(m\u2212n) = 8, giving m \u2212 n = 3, so m = 6. Check: 64 \u2212 8 = 56.
Step 4: P = (6, 3) and Q = (\u22122, \u22123). Distance = \u221A((6 + 2)\xB2 + (3 + 3)\xB2) = \u221A(64 + 36) = \u221A100 = 10.
Answer: 10, option (c).`},{pn:6,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"An organization awarded 48 medals in event \u2018A\u2019, 25 in event \u2018B\u2019 and 18 in event \u2018C\u2019. If these medals went to total 60 men and only five men got medals in all the three events, then, how many received medals in exactly two of three events?",o:["10","9","21","15"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Use n(A \u222A B \u222A C) = n(A) + n(B) + n(C) \u2212 [sum of pairwise overlaps] + n(A \u2229 B \u2229 C). Here the union is 60 men.
Step 2: 60 = 48 + 25 + 18 \u2212 S + 5 = 96 \u2212 S, so S = 36, where S = n(A\u2229B) + n(B\u2229C) + n(C\u2229A).
Step 3: Each pairwise overlap counts the 5 men who won all three events. So in S, those 5 men are counted 3 times (once in each pair).
Step 4: Men with exactly two events = S \u2212 3 \xD7 5 = 36 \u2212 15 = 21.
Answer: 21, option (c).`},{pn:7,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let S = \uFF5Bx \u2208 [\u22126, 3] \u2212 \uFF5B\u22122, 2\uFF5D : (|x + 3| \u2212 1)/(|x| \u2212 2) \u2265 0\uFF5D and T = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u2124 : x\xB2 \u2212 7|x| + 9 \u2264 0\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in S \u2229 T is",o:["7","5","4","3"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Find T first. Put |x| = u: u\xB2 \u2212 7u + 9 \u2264 0 gives u between (7 \u2212 \u221A13)/2 \u2248 1.70 and (7 + \u221A13)/2 \u2248 5.30.
Step 2: Integers x with 1.70 \u2264 |x| \u2264 5.30 are |x| = 2, 3, 4, 5, i.e. x = \xB12, \xB13, \xB14, \xB15.
Step 3: S needs x in [\u22126, 3] and x \u2260 \xB12. This removes 2, \u22122, 4 and 5 (they are outside [\u22126, 3] or excluded). Candidates left: \u22125, \u22124, \u22123, 3.
Step 4: Test each in (|x + 3| \u2212 1)/(|x| \u2212 2): x = \u22125: (2 \u2212 1)/(5 \u2212 2) = 1/3 \u2265 0 yes. x = \u22124: (1 \u2212 1)/2 = 0 yes. x = \u22123: (0 \u2212 1)/1 = \u22121 no. x = 3: (6 \u2212 1)/1 = 5 yes.
Step 5: So S \u2229 T = \uFF5B\u22125, \u22124, 3\uFF5D, which has 3 elements (checked by a short program).
Answer: 3, option (d).`},{pn:8,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x + 1| < 2\uFF5D and B = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x \u2212 1| \u2265 2\uFF5D. Then which one of the following statements is not true?",o:["A \u2212 B = (\u22121, 1)","B \u2212 A = \u211D \u2212 (\u22123, 1)","A \u2229 B = (\u22123, \u22121]","A \u222A B = \u211D \u2212 [1, 3)"],c:1,s:`Note: the printed key says (d); working gives (b), because (d) is actually true.
Step 1: |x + 1| < 2 gives \u22123 < x < 1, so A = (\u22123, 1).
Step 2: |x \u2212 1| \u2265 2 gives x \u2264 \u22121 or x \u2265 3, so B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22121] \u222A [3, \u221E).
Step 3: A \u2212 B = points of A not in B = (\u22121, 1). So (a) is true.
Step 4: A \u2229 B = (\u22123, \u22121]. So (c) is true.
Step 5: A \u222A B = (\u2212\u221E, 1) \u222A [3, \u221E) = \u211D \u2212 [1, 3). So (d) is true.
Step 6: B \u2212 A = (\u2212\u221E, \u22123] \u222A [3, \u221E) = \u211D \u2212 (\u22123, 3), not \u211D \u2212 (\u22123, 1). So (b) is the false statement.
Answer: option (b).`},{pn:9,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"Out of all the patients in a hospital 89% are found to be suffering from heart ailment and 98% are suffering from lungs infection. If K% of them are suffering from both ailments, then K can not belong to the set",o:["\uFF5B79, 81, 83, 85\uFF5D","\uFF5B84, 87, 90, 93\uFF5D","\uFF5B80, 83, 86, 89\uFF5D","\uFF5B84, 86, 88, 90\uFF5D"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Let H = heart, L = lungs. n(H \u222A L) \u2264 100, so K = 89 + 98 \u2212 n(H \u222A L) \u2265 187 \u2212 100 = 87.
Step 2: The overlap cannot be bigger than the smaller set, so K \u2264 89.
Step 3: So K can be any value from 87 to 89.
Step 4: We need the set that has no value in 87 to 89. (b) has 87, (c) has 89, (d) has 88, but (a) = \uFF5B79, 81, 83, 85\uFF5D has none.
Answer: option (a).`},{pn:10,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D | 2x\xB2 + 2y\xB2 \u2212 2x \u2212 2y = 1\uFF5D, B = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D | 4x\xB2 + 4y\xB2 \u2212 16y + 7 = 0\uFF5D and C = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D | x\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2212 4x \u2212 2y + 5 \u2264 r\xB2\uFF5D. Then the minimum value of |r| such that A \u222A B \u2286 C is equal to",o:["1 + \u221A5","(3 + 2\u221A5)/2","(3 + \u221A10)/2","(2 + \u221A10)/2"],c:1,s:`Step 1: A: x\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2212 x \u2212 y = 1/2. Centre (1/2, 1/2), radius\xB2 = 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/4 = 1, so radius 1.
Step 2: B: x\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2212 4y + 7/4 = 0. Centre (0, 2), radius\xB2 = 4 \u2212 7/4 = 9/4, so radius 3/2.
Step 3: C: (x \u2212 2)\xB2 + (y \u2212 1)\xB2 \u2264 r\xB2, a disc with centre (2, 1) and radius |r|.
Step 4: The disc must cover every point of both circles. The farthest point of a circle from (2, 1) is at distance (distance to its centre) + (its radius).
Step 5: For A: distance from (2, 1) to (1/2, 1/2) = \u221A(9/4 + 1/4) = \u221A10/2 \u2248 1.58, so need 1.58 + 1 \u2248 2.58. For B: distance to (0, 2) = \u221A5, so need \u221A5 + 3/2 = (3 + 2\u221A5)/2 \u2248 3.74.
Step 6: The larger of the two is needed, so the minimum |r| = (3 + 2\u221A5)/2.
Answer: (3 + 2\u221A5)/2, option (b).`},{pn:11,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"In a school, there are three types of games to be played. Some of the students play two types of games, but none play all the three games. Which Venn diagrams can justify the above statement? (Three diagrams P, Q, R are drawn: in each a large circle meets two other circles.)",o:["None of these","P and Q","P and R","Q and R"],c:0,fig:{w:360,h:100,alt:"Three Venn diagrams labelled P, Q and R. In each, a large circle contains or meets two other circles that overlap each other.",d:[["c",60,45,38],["c",48,45,20],["c",72,45,20],["t",60,95,"P","m"],["c",180,45,37],["c",157,45,24],["c",203,45,24],["t",180,95,"Q","m"],["c",300,45,37],["c",276,45,23],["c",316,45,20],["t",300,95,"R","m"]]},s:`Step 1: The statement needs three sets (the three games) in which some pairs overlap, but the region common to all three is empty.
Step 2: Diagram P: the two small circles overlap inside the big circle, so a region lies in all three circles. Not allowed.
Step 3: Diagram Q: the two side circles also cross each other inside the big circle, so again a region lies in all three. Not allowed.
Step 4: Diagram R: the two smaller circles overlap each other inside the big circle, giving a common region of all three. Not allowed.
Step 5: Every diagram has a region common to all three circles, so none of them shows "nobody plays all three".
Answer: None of these, option (a).`},{pn:12,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let S\u2081, S\u2082 and S\u2083 be three sets defined as S\u2081 = \uFF5Bz \u2208 \u2102 : |z \u2212 1| \u2264 \u221A2\uFF5D, S\u2082 = \uFF5Bz \u2208 \u2102 : Re((1 \u2212 i)z) \u2265 1\uFF5D, S\u2083 = \uFF5Bz \u2208 \u2102 : Im(z) \u2264 1\uFF5D. Then the set S\u2081 \u2229 S\u2082 \u2229 S\u2083",o:["has exactly two elements","has infinitely many points","has exactly three elements","is a singleton"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Write z = x + iy. S\u2081 is the disc with centre (1, 0) and radius \u221A2.
Step 2: (1 \u2212 i)(x + iy) = (x + y) + i(y \u2212 x), so Re = x + y. S\u2082 is the half-plane x + y \u2265 1.
Step 3: S\u2083 is the half-plane y \u2264 1.
Step 4: Check one point: (1, 0.5). Distance from (1, 0) is 0.5 \u2264 \u221A2, x + y = 1.5 \u2265 1, y = 0.5 \u2264 1. So it is in all three.
Step 5: Points near (1, 0.5) also satisfy all three conditions strictly, so the common region has some area, not just a few points.
Answer: infinitely many points, option (b).`},{pn:13,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Consider the two sets : A = \uFF5Bm \u2208 \u211D : both the roots of x\xB2 \u2212 (m + 1)x + m + 4 = 0 are real\uFF5D and B = [\u22123, 5). Which of the following is not true?",o:["A \u2212 B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22123) \u222A (5, \u221E)","A \u2229 B = \uFF5B\u22123\uFF5D","B \u2212 A = (\u22123, 5)","A \u222A B = \u211D"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Real roots need discriminant \u2265 0: (m + 1)\xB2 \u2212 4(m + 4) \u2265 0.
Step 2: Expand: m\xB2 \u2212 2m \u2212 15 \u2265 0, i.e. (m \u2212 5)(m + 3) \u2265 0, so m \u2264 \u22123 or m \u2265 5. A = (\u2212\u221E, \u22123] \u222A [5, \u221E).
Step 3: B = [\u22123, 5). A \u2212 B removes \u22123 (which is in B) and keeps 5 (which is not in B): A \u2212 B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22123) \u222A [5, \u221E).
Step 4: Option (a) says (5, \u221E), leaving out 5, so (a) is not true.
Step 5: Check the others: A \u2229 B = \uFF5B\u22123\uFF5D, B \u2212 A = (\u22123, 5), A \u222A B = \u211D are all true.
Answer: option (a).`},{pn:14,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"A survey shows that 63% of the people in a city read newspaper A, whereas 76% read newspaper B. If x% of the people read both the newspapers, then a possible value of x can be",o:["65","55","37","29"],c:1,s:`Step 1: x can be at most the smaller percentage, so x \u2264 63.
Step 2: Together A and B cannot be more than 100%: 63 + 76 \u2212 x \u2264 100, so x \u2265 39.
Step 3: So 39 \u2264 x \u2264 63.
Step 4: Among the options only 55 is in this range (65 is too big, 37 and 29 are too small).
Answer: 55, option (b).`},{pn:15,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"Let \u22C3\u1D62\u208C\u2081\u2075\u2070 X\u1D62 = \u22C3\u1D62\u208C\u2081\u207F Y\u1D62 = T, where each X\u1D62 contains 10 elements and each Y\u1D62 contains 5 elements. If each element of the set T is an element of exactly 20 of sets X\u1D62's and exactly 6 of sets Y\u1D62's, then n is equal to",o:["45","30","50","15"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Count pairs (element, X-set containing it) in two ways. By sets: 50 sets \xD7 10 elements = 500 pairs.
Step 2: By elements: each of the |T| elements is in 20 of the X-sets, so 20|T| = 500 and |T| = 25.
Step 3: Do the same for the Y-sets: n sets \xD7 5 elements = 5n pairs.
Step 4: By elements: each of the 25 elements is in 6 Y-sets, so 25 \xD7 6 = 150 pairs.
Step 5: 5n = 150, so n = 30.
Answer: 30, option (b).`},{pn:16,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"A survey shows that 73% of the persons working in an office like coffee, whereas 65% like tea. If x denotes the percentage of them, who like both coffee and tea, then x cannot be",o:["63","36","54","38"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Both-likers cannot be more than those liking tea (the smaller group): x \u2264 65.
Step 2: 73 + 65 \u2212 x \u2264 100 (union cannot exceed 100%), so x \u2265 38.
Step 3: So x lies from 38 to 65.
Step 4: Options: 63 yes, 54 yes, 38 yes, but 36 is below 38.
Answer: x cannot be 36, option (b).`},{pn:17,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"If A = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x| < 2\uFF5D and B = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x \u2212 2| > 3\uFF5D ; then",o:["A \u2212 B = [\u22121, 2)","B \u2212 A = \u211D \u2212 (\u22122, 5)","A \u222A B = \u211D \u2212 (2, 5)","A \u2229 B = (\u22122, \u22121)"],c:[0,3],s:`Note: the printed key says (b); working gives (a) and (d) for the strict inequality as printed (with \u2265 the original would give (b)).
Step 1: |x| < 2 gives A = (\u22122, 2).
Step 2: |x \u2212 2| > 3 gives x \u2212 2 > 3 or x \u2212 2 < \u22123, so B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22121) \u222A (5, \u221E).
Step 3: A \u2229 B = (\u22122, \u22121). So (d) is true.
Step 4: A \u2212 B = (\u22122, 2) with (\u22122, \u22121) removed = [\u22121, 2). So (a) is true.
Step 5: B \u2212 A = (\u2212\u221E, \u22122] \u222A (5, \u221E) = \u211D \u2212 (\u22122, 5], which differs from (b) at the point 5. So (b) is false.
Step 6: A \u222A B = (\u2212\u221E, 2) \u222A (5, \u221E) = \u211D \u2212 [2, 5], not \u211D \u2212 (2, 5). So (c) is false.
Answer: options (a) and (d).`},{pn:18,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"Two newspapers A and B are published in a city. It is known that 25% of the city population reads A and 20% reads B while 8% reads both A and B. Further, 30% of those who read A but not B look into advertisements and 40% of those who read B but not A also look into advertisements, while 50% of those who read both A and B look into advertisements. Then the percentage of the population who look into advertisements is",o:["13.5","13.9","13","12.8"],c:1,s:`Step 1: A only = 25 \u2212 8 = 17% of the population. B only = 20 \u2212 8 = 12%. Both = 8%.
Step 2: Advertisement readers from A only: 30% of 17% = 5.1%.
Step 3: From B only: 40% of 12% = 4.8%.
Step 4: From both: 50% of 8% = 4%.
Step 5: Add: 5.1 + 4.8 + 4 = 13.9%.
Answer: 13.9, option (b).`},{pn:19,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A, B and C be sets such that \u03C6 \u2260 A \u2229 B \u2286 C. Then which of the following statements is not true?",o:["B \u2229 C \u2260 \u03C6","If (A \u2212 C) \u2286 B, then A \u2286 B","(C \u222A A) \u2229 (C \u222A B) = C","If (A \u2212 B) \u2286 C, then A \u2286 C"],c:1,s:`Step 1: (a) A \u2229 B is non-empty and lies inside both B and C, so B \u2229 C contains it and is non-empty. True.
Step 2: (c) (C \u222A A) \u2229 (C \u222A B) = C \u222A (A \u2229 B) by the distributive law. Since A \u2229 B \u2286 C, this equals C. True.
Step 3: (d) A = (A \u2229 B) \u222A (A \u2212 B). Both parts are inside C, so A \u2286 C. True.
Step 4: (b) Try A = \uFF5B1, 2\uFF5D, B = \uFF5B1\uFF5D, C = \uFF5B1, 2\uFF5D. Then A \u2229 B = \uFF5B1\uFF5D \u2286 C and A \u2212 C = \u2205 \u2286 B, but A is not a subset of B.
Step 5: So (b) can fail, which makes it the statement that is not true.
Answer: option (b).`},{pn:20,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"In a class of 140 students numbered 1 to 140, all even numbered students opted Mathematics course, those whose number is divisible by 3 opted Physics course and those whose number is divisible by 5 opted Chemistry course. Then the number of students who did not opt for any of the three courses is",o:["102","38","1","42"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Even numbers: 140/2 = 70. Multiples of 3: 46. Multiples of 5: 28.
Step 2: Multiples of 6: 23. Of 10: 14. Of 15: 9. Of 30: 4.
Step 3: Union = 70 + 46 + 28 \u2212 23 \u2212 14 \u2212 9 + 4 = 102 (inclusion-exclusion).
Step 4: Students with no course = 140 \u2212 102 = 38 (also checked by a short program).
Answer: 38, option (b).`},{pn:21,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let S = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, \u2026, 100\uFF5D. The number of non-empty subsets A of S such that the product of elements in A is even is",o:["2\u2075\u2070(2\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1)","2\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1","2\u2075\u2070 + 1","2\xB9\u2070\u2070 \u2212 1"],c:0,s:`Step 1: S has 100 elements, so it has 2\xB9\u2070\u2070 subsets, of which 2\xB9\u2070\u2070 \u2212 1 are non-empty.
Step 2: The product is odd only when every chosen element is odd. S has 50 odd numbers.
Step 3: Non-empty subsets made only of odd numbers: 2\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1.
Step 4: Even-product subsets = (2\xB9\u2070\u2070 \u2212 1) \u2212 (2\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1) = 2\xB9\u2070\u2070 \u2212 2\u2075\u2070.
Step 5: Factor: 2\u2075\u2070(2\u2075\u2070 \u2212 1).
Answer: option (a).`},{pn:22,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Two sets A and B as under : A = \uFF5B(a, b) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D : |a \u2212 5| < 1 and |b \u2212 5| < 1\uFF5D; B = \uFF5B(a, b) \u2208 \u211D\xD7\u211D : 4(a \u2212 6)\xB2 + 9(b \u2212 5)\xB2 \u2264 36\uFF5D. Then",o:["neither A \u2282 B nor B \u2282 A","B \u2282 A","A \u2282 B","A \u2229 B = \u03C6 (an empty set)"],c:2,s:`Step 1: |a \u2212 5| < 1 and |b \u2212 5| < 1 describe the open square 4 < a < 6, 4 < b < 6. That is A.
Step 2: Divide the second condition by 36: (a \u2212 6)\xB2/9 + (b \u2212 5)\xB2/4 \u2264 1. B is the inside of an ellipse with centre (6, 5), spreading 3 along a (3 to 9) and 2 along b (3 to 7).
Step 3: A point is in B when 4(a \u2212 6)\xB2 + 9(b \u2212 5)\xB2 \u2264 36. The square corner farthest from (6, 5) is such as (4, 4): 4 \xD7 4 + 9 \xD7 1 = 25 \u2264 36. Same for (4, 6): 25 \u2264 36.
Step 4: Every other point of the square is closer to the centre than a farthest corner, so it also satisfies the inequality. So A \u2282 B.
Step 5: B is not inside A (for example (9, 5) is in B but not in A).
Answer: A \u2282 B, option (c).`},{pn:23,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:`In a certain town, 25% of the families own a phone and 15% own a car, 65% families own neither a phone nor a car and 2000 families own both a car and a phone. Consider the following three statements :
(1) 5% families own both a car and a phone.
(2) 35% families own either a car or a phone.
(3) 40,000 families live in the town.
Then`,o:["only (1) and (2) are correct","only (1) and (3) are correct","only (2) and (3) are correct","all (1), (2) and (3) are correct"],c:3,s:`Step 1: 65% own neither, so 100 \u2212 65 = 35% own a phone or a car. Statement (2) is true.
Step 2: n(P \u222A C) = n(P) + n(C) \u2212 n(P \u2229 C): 35 = 25 + 15 \u2212 both, so both = 5%. Statement (1) is true.
Step 3: 5% of the families = 2000, so all families = 2000/0.05 = 40,000. Statement (3) is true.
Answer: all three are correct, option (d).`},{pn:24,y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"If A, B and C are three sets such that A \u2229 B = A \u2229 C and A \u222A B = A \u222A C, then",o:["A = C","B = C","A \u2229 B = \u03C6","A = B"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Take any x in B. We show x is in C.
Step 2: If x is in A, then x \u2208 A \u2229 B = A \u2229 C, so x \u2208 C.
Step 3: If x is not in A, then x \u2208 A \u222A B = A \u222A C. Since x is not in A, x must be in C.
Step 4: So B \u2286 C. The same argument with B and C swapped gives C \u2286 B.
Step 5: Hence B = C. (A counterexample for the others: A = \uFF5B1\uFF5D, B = C = \uFF5B2\uFF5D gives A \u2260 B, A \u2260 C.)
Answer: B = C, option (b).`},{pn:25,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The set S = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, \u2026, 12\uFF5D is to be partitioned into three sets A, B, C of equal size. Thus A \u222A B \u222A C = S, A \u2229 B = B \u2229 C = A \u2229 C = \u03C6. The number of ways to partition S is",o:["12!/(4!)\xB3","12!/(4!)\u2074","12!/(3!(4!)\xB3)","12!/(3!(4!)\u2074)"],c:2,s:`Note: the printed key says (a); working gives (c), since the sets are unlabelled.
Step 1: Each set has 12/3 = 4 elements.
Step 2: If A, B, C were named and in order: choose 4 for A (12C4), 4 of the rest for B (8C4), the last 4 go to C. That is 12!/(4!\xB74!\xB74!) = 12!/(4!)\xB3.
Step 3: But the sets are not named; the same three groups in a different order are the same partition. Each partition was counted 3! times.
Step 4: Divide by 3!: 12!/(3!(4!)\xB3) = 5775 (checked by a short program).
Answer: option (c).`},{pn:26,y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:`Let S = \uFF5Ba + b\u221A2 : a, b \u2208 \u2124\uFF5D, T\u2081 = \uFF5B(\u22121 + \u221A2)\u207F : n \u2208 \u2115\uFF5D and T\u2082 = \uFF5B(1 + \u221A2)\u207F : n \u2208 \u2115\uFF5D. Then which of the following statements is (are) TRUE?
(a) \u2124 \u222A T\u2081 \u222A T\u2082 \u2282 S
(b) T\u2081 \u2229 (0, 1/2024) = \u2205, where \u2205 denotes the empty set.
(c) T\u2082 \u2229 (2024, \u221E) \u2260 \u2205
(d) For any given a, b \u2208 \u2124, cos(\u03C0(a + b\u221A2)) + i sin(\u03C0(a + b\u221A2)) \u2208 \u2124 if and only if b = 0, where i = \u221A\u22121.`,o:["\u2124 \u222A T\u2081 \u222A T\u2082 \u2282 S","T\u2081 \u2229 (0, 1/2024) = \u2205","T\u2082 \u2229 (2024, \u221E) \u2260 \u2205","cos(\u03C0(a + b\u221A2)) + i sin(\u03C0(a + b\u221A2)) \u2208 \u2124 if and only if b = 0"],c:[0,2,3],s:`Step 1: (a) Every integer a is a + 0\xB7\u221A2, so \u2124 \u2282 S. Also (1 + \u221A2)\u207F expands by the binomial theorem into (integer) + (integer)\xB7\u221A2, and the same holds for (\u22121 + \u221A2)\u207F. So T\u2081 and T\u2082 are inside S and (a) is TRUE.
Step 2: (b) 0 < \u221A2 \u2212 1 < 1 (it is about 0.414), so (\u22121 + \u221A2)\u207F = (\u221A2 \u2212 1)\u207F for n large becomes as small as we like and is positive. So some term lies in (0, 1/2024), the intersection is not empty and (b) is FALSE.
Step 3: (c) 1 + \u221A2 > 1 (about 2.414), so (1 + \u221A2)\u207F grows without limit and eventually exceeds 2024. So T\u2082 \u2229 (2024, \u221E) \u2260 \u2205 and (c) is TRUE.
Step 4: (d) The number cos \u03B8 + i sin \u03B8 is the point e^\uFF5Bi\u03B8\uFF5D; with \u03B8 = \u03C0(a + b\u221A2). If b = 0 it equals cos(a\u03C0) + i sin(a\u03C0) = (\u22121)\u1D43, an integer. So "if" holds.
Step 5: Conversely, cos \u03B8 + i sin \u03B8 has modulus 1, so if it is an integer it must be 1 or \u22121. Then sin \u03B8 = 0, so \u03B8 is a multiple of \u03C0, which means a + b\u221A2 is an integer. Since \u221A2 is irrational this forces b = 0. So "only if" holds and (d) is TRUE.
Answer: (a), (c), (d).`},{pn:27,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"In a survey of 220 students of a higher secondary school, it was found that at least 125 and at most 130 students studied Mathematics; at least 85 and at most 95 studied Physics; at least 75 and at most 90 studied Chemistry; 30 studied both Physics and Chemistry; 50 studied both Chemistry and Mathematics; 40 studied both Mathematics and Physics and 10 studied none of these subjects. Let m and n respectively be the least and the most number of students who studied all the three subjects. Then m + n is equal to ______.",n:45,s:`Step 1: 10 students studied none, so the union of the three subjects has 220 \u2212 10 = 210 students.
Step 2: Let x be the number who studied all three. Inclusion\u2013exclusion: 210 = M + P + C \u2212 (30 + 50 + 40) + x, where M, P, C are the sizes of the subject sets.
Step 3: So M + P + C = 330 \u2212 x, i.e. x = 330 \u2212 (M + P + C).
Step 4: The smallest possible M + P + C is 125 + 85 + 75 = 285 and the largest is 130 + 95 + 90 = 315. This gives 15 \u2264 x \u2264 45.
Step 5: But all three together is part of "Physics and Chemistry", which has only 30 students, so x \u2264 30. Hence the most is n = 30 (it works with M + P + C = 300).
Step 6: The least is m = 15 (it works with M = 130, P = 95, C = 90 giving 315).
Step 7: m + n = 15 + 30.
Answer: 45`},{pn:28,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"If S = \uFF5Ba \u2208 \u211D : |2a \u2212 1| = 3[a] + 2\uFF5Ba\uFF5D\uFF5D, where [t] denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to t and \uFF5Bt\uFF5D represents the fractional part of t, then 72 \u03A3(a \u2208 S) a is equal to ______.",n:18,s:`Step 1: Write a = n + f where n = [a] is an integer and f = \uFF5Ba\uFF5D lies in 0 \u2264 f < 1. The right side becomes 3n + 2f.
Step 2: Case 1, 2a \u2212 1 \u2265 0: then 2n + 2f \u2212 1 = 3n + 2f, which gives n = \u22121. But then a = \u22121 + f < 0, so 2a \u2212 1 < 0, a contradiction. No solution here.
Step 3: Case 2, 2a \u2212 1 < 0: then 1 \u2212 2n \u2212 2f = 3n + 2f, so 4f = 1 \u2212 5n and f = (1 \u2212 5n)/4.
Step 4: We need 0 \u2264 f < 1, i.e. 0 \u2264 1 \u2212 5n < 4. This gives n \u2264 1/5 and n > \u22123/5, so the only integer is n = 0.
Step 5: Then f = 1/4 and a = 1/4. Check: |2\xB7(1/4) \u2212 1| = 1/2 and 3\xB70 + 2\xB7(1/4) = 1/2. It works, so S = \uFF5B1/4\uFF5D.
Step 6: 72 \xD7 (1/4) = 18.
Answer: 18`},{pn:29,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The number of elements in the set S = \uFF5B(x, y, z) : x, y, z \u2208 \u2124, x + 2y + 3z = 42, x, y, z \u2265 0\uFF5D equals ______.",n:169,s:`Step 1: Fix z. Then x + 2y = 42 \u2212 3z, and x = 42 \u2212 3z \u2212 2y must be \u2265 0, so y can be 0, 1, ..., \u230A(42 \u2212 3z)/2\u230B. That gives \u230A(42 \u2212 3z)/2\u230B + 1 choices of (x, y). Also z runs from 0 to 14.
Step 2: Even z = 2t (t = 0 to 7): (42 \u2212 6t)/2 + 1 = 22 \u2212 3t, i.e. 22, 19, 16, 13, 10, 7, 4, 1. Their sum is 92.
Step 3: Odd z = 2t + 1 (t = 0 to 6): 42 \u2212 3z = 39 \u2212 6t, which is odd, so the count is (38 \u2212 6t)/2 + 1 = 20 \u2212 3t, i.e. 20, 17, 14, 11, 8, 5, 2. Their sum is 77.
Step 4: Total = 92 + 77 = 169 (confirmed by a quick computer count).
Answer: 169`},{pn:30,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let the set C = \uFF5B(x, y) | x\xB2 \u2212 2\u02B8 = 2023, x, y \u2208 \u2115\uFF5D. Then \u03A3(x, y) \u2208 C (x + y) is equal to ______.",n:46,s:`Step 1: We need x\xB2 = 2023 + 2\u02B8 with x, y natural numbers.
Step 2: Look at remainders mod 4. 2023 = 4\xB7505 + 3, so 2023 \u2261 3 (mod 4). If y \u2265 2, then 2\u02B8 \u2261 0 (mod 4), so x\xB2 \u2261 3 (mod 4), but a square leaves remainder 0 or 1 only. Impossible.
Step 3: So y = 1 (the only other natural number below 2). Then x\xB2 = 2023 + 2 = 2025 = 45\xB2, so x = 45.
Step 4: C = \uFF5B(45, 1)\uFF5D, and x + y = 46.
Answer: 46`},{pn:31,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Venn diagrams and counting",q:"A group of 40 students appeared in an examination of 3 subjects - Mathematics, Physics and Chemistry. It was found that all students passed in atleast one of the subjects, 20 students passed in Mathematics, 25 students passed in Physics, 16 students passed in Chemistry, atmost 11 students passed in both Mathematics and Physics, atmost 15 students passed in both Physics and Chemistry, atmost 15 students passed in both Mathematics and Chemistry. The maximum number of students passed in all the three subjects is ______.",n:10,s:`Step 1: Let x be the number passing all three, and let the pair counts be MP, PC, MC (each includes the x students).
Step 2: Everyone passed at least one, so 40 = 20 + 25 + 16 \u2212 (MP + PC + MC) + x. Hence MP + PC + MC = 21 + x.
Step 3: Each pair count is at least x, so 3x \u2264 MP + PC + MC = 21 + x, which gives 2x \u2264 21 and x \u2264 10.5. So x \u2264 10.
Step 4: Check x = 10 can happen: take MP = 11, PC = 10, MC = 10 (sum 31 = 21 + 10, and all within the limits 11, 15, 15).
Step 5: Only-M = 20 \u2212 11 \u2212 10 + 10 = 9, only-P = 25 \u2212 11 \u2212 10 + 10 = 14, only-C = 16 \u2212 10 \u2212 10 + 10 = 6, exactly MP only = 1, others 0, all three = 10. Total = 9 + 14 + 6 + 1 + 10 = 40. All numbers are non-negative, so it works.
Answer: 10`},{pn:32,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The number of elements in the set \uFF5Bn \u2208 \u2124 : |n\xB2 \u2212 10n + 19| < 6\uFF5D is ______.",n:6,s:`Step 1: |n\xB2 \u2212 10n + 19| < 6 means \u22126 < n\xB2 \u2212 10n + 19 < 6. Solve both parts.
Step 2: Left part: n\xB2 \u2212 10n + 25 > 0, i.e. (n \u2212 5)\xB2 > 0, true for every n except n = 5.
Step 3: Right part: n\xB2 \u2212 10n + 13 < 0. The roots are n = 5 \xB1 \u221A12 \u2248 5 \xB1 3.46, so 1.54 < n < 8.46.
Step 4: Integers in this range: 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8. Remove n = 5.
Step 5: Left: 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, which is 6 numbers.
Answer: 6`},{pn:33,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The number of elements in the set \uFF5Bn \u2208 \u2115 : 10 \u2264 n \u2264 100 and 3\u207F \u2212 3 is a multiple of 7\uFF5D is ______.",n:15,s:`Step 1: 3\u207F \u2212 3 is a multiple of 7 means 3\u207F \u2261 3 (mod 7). Divide by 3 (allowed since 3 and 7 are coprime): 3\u207F\u207B\xB9 \u2261 1 (mod 7).
Step 2: Powers of 3 mod 7: 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1, then it repeats. So 3\u1D4F \u2261 1 (mod 7) exactly when k is a multiple of 6.
Step 3: So n \u2212 1 = 6j, i.e. n = 6j + 1.
Step 4: In 10 \u2264 n \u2264 100 these are 13, 19, 25, ..., 97 (7 is below 10).
Step 5: Count = (97 \u2212 13)/6 + 1 = 14 + 1 = 15.
Answer: 15`},{pn:34,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let \u03BB \u2208 \u211D and let the equation E be |x|\xB2 \u2212 2|x| + |\u03BB \u2212 3| = 0. Then the largest element in the set S = \uFF5Bx + \u03BB : x is an integer solution of E\uFF5D is ______.",n:5,s:`Step 1: Put t = |x| (an integer \u2265 0). The equation is t\xB2 \u2212 2t + |\u03BB \u2212 3| = 0, so |\u03BB \u2212 3| = 2t \u2212 t\xB2 = t(2 \u2212 t).
Step 2: Since |\u03BB \u2212 3| \u2265 0, we need t(2 \u2212 t) \u2265 0, so t = 0, 1 or 2.
Step 3: t = 0 or t = 2 gives |\u03BB \u2212 3| = 0, so \u03BB = 3 and x = 0, 2, \u22122. Then x + \u03BB = 3, 5, 1.
Step 4: t = 1 gives |\u03BB \u2212 3| = 1, so \u03BB = 4 or 2 and x = \xB11. Then x + \u03BB = 5, 3 (for \u03BB = 4) and 3, 1 (for \u03BB = 2).
Step 5: All possible values of x + \u03BB are 1, 3, 5. The largest is 5.
Answer: 5`},{pn:35,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\uFF5D. Define B = \uFF5BT \u2286 A : either 1 \u2209 T or 2 \u2208 T\uFF5D and C = \uFF5BT \u2286 A : the sum of all the elements of T is a prime number\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in the set B \u222A C is ______.",n:107,s:`Note: the printed key says 27; working gives 107 (the printed keys of Q35 and Q38 look swapped).
Step 1: A has 7 elements, so it has 2\u2077 = 128 subsets.
Step 2: The subsets NOT in B are those with 1 \u2208 T and 2 \u2209 T. The other 5 elements are free: 2\u2075 = 32 subsets. So |B| = 128 \u2212 32 = 96.
Step 3: B \u222A C = B plus those subsets of C that are not in B. So we count subsets with 1 \u2208 T, 2 \u2209 T and prime sum.
Step 4: Such a T is \uFF5B1\uFF5D plus a subset U of \uFF5B3, 4, 5, 6, 7\uFF5D, with sum 1 + (sum of U) prime. Go through the 32 subsets U. The totals that are prime are: \uFF5B1,4\uFF5D=5, \uFF5B1,6\uFF5D=7, \uFF5B1,3,7\uFF5D=11, \uFF5B1,4,6\uFF5D=11, \uFF5B1,3,4,5\uFF5D=13, \uFF5B1,5,7\uFF5D=13, \uFF5B1,4,5,7\uFF5D=17, \uFF5B1,3,6,7\uFF5D=17, \uFF5B1,3,4,5,6\uFF5D=19, \uFF5B1,5,6,7\uFF5D=19, \uFF5B1,4,5,6,7\uFF5D=23. That is 11 subsets.
Step 5: |B \u222A C| = 96 + 11 = 107.
Answer: 107`},{pn:36,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B3, 6, 7, 9\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in the set \uFF5BC \u2286 A : C \u2229 B \u2260 \u2205\uFF5D is ______.",n:112,s:`Step 1: Count all subsets of A and subtract the ones that do not meet B. A has 7 elements, so 2\u2077 = 128 subsets.
Step 2: C \u2229 B = \u2205 means C uses none of the elements of A that are also in B. A \u2229 B = \uFF5B3, 6, 7\uFF5D (9 is not in A).
Step 3: So C must be a subset of the remaining 7 \u2212 3 = 4 elements \uFF5B1, 2, 4, 5\uFF5D: 2\u2074 = 16 subsets.
Step 4: Required number = 128 \u2212 16 = 112.
Answer: 112`},{pn:37,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let S = \uFF5B4, 6, 9\uFF5D and T = \uFF5B9, 10, 11, ..., 1000\uFF5D. If A = \uFF5Ba\u2081 + a\u2082 + ... + a\u2096 : k \u2208 \u2115, a\u2081, a\u2082, a\u2083, ..., a\u2096 \u2208 S\uFF5D, then the sum of all the elements in the set T \u2212 A is equal to ______.",n:11,s:`Step 1: A is the set of all numbers that can be written as a sum of one or more 4s, 6s and 9s (repeats allowed). T \u2212 A is the numbers in T that cannot be written like that.
Step 2: Check the start of T: 9 = 9, 10 = 4 + 6, 11 = ? (4, 6, 9, 4 + 4 = 8, 4 + 6 = 10, 6 + 6 = 12 ... no way to get 11), 12 = 4 + 4 + 4, 13 = 4 + 9, 14 = 4 + 4 + 6, 15 = 6 + 9.
Step 3: So 11 is not in A.
Step 4: Every number from 12 onward can be built: 12, 13, 14, 15 are all in A, and adding 4 to a number in A gives another one in A, which reaches every number \u2265 12 (12, 13, 14, 15, then +4 repeatedly).
Step 5: So T \u2212 A = \uFF5B11\uFF5D and its sum is 11.
Answer: 11`},{pn:38,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let S = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u2115 \xD7 \u2115 : 9(x \u2212 3)\xB2 + 16(y \u2212 4)\xB2 \u2264 144\uFF5D and T = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D \xD7 \u211D : (x \u2212 7)\xB2 + (y \u2212 4)\xB2 \u2264 36\uFF5D. Then n(S \u2229 T) is equal to ______.",n:27,s:`Note: the printed key says 107; working gives 27 (the printed keys of Q35 and Q38 look swapped).
Step 1: Divide the first condition by 144: (x \u2212 3)\xB2/16 + (y \u2212 4)\xB2/9 \u2264 1, an ellipse centred at (3, 4). So x \u2212 3 is between \u22124 and 4 and y \u2212 4 is between \u22123 and 3. With x, y natural numbers, x runs 1 to 7.
Step 2: Points of S for each x (y values): x = 1: 2 to 6; x = 2: 2 to 6; x = 3: 1 to 7; x = 4: 2 to 6; x = 5: 2 to 6; x = 6: 3, 4, 5; x = 7: 4. (Example x = 6: 9\xB79 + 16(y \u2212 4)\xB2 \u2264 144 gives (y \u2212 4)\xB2 \u2264 63/16, so |y \u2212 4| \u2264 1.)
Step 3: T is the circle with centre (7, 4) and radius 6. Check each point: for x = 1, (x \u2212 7)\xB2 = 36, so only y = 4 fits. For x = 2: 25 + (y \u2212 4)\xB2 \u2264 36 allows |y \u2212 4| \u2264 3, so all y 2 to 6 fit.
Step 4: For x = 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 the horizontal distance is at most 4, so (x \u2212 7)\xB2 \u2264 16 and every point of S in those columns lies in the circle (the vertical gap is at most 3, so 16 + 9 = 25 \u2264 36).
Step 5: Count: x = 1: 1, x = 2: 5, x = 3: 7, x = 4: 5, x = 5: 5, x = 6: 3, x = 7: 1. Total = 1 + 5 + 7 + 5 + 5 + 3 + 1 = 27.
Answer: 27`},{pn:39,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The sum of all the elements of the set \uFF5B\u03B1 \u2208 \uFF5B1, 2, ..., 100\uFF5D : HCF(\u03B1, 24) = 1\uFF5D is ______.",n:1633,s:`Step 1: 24 = 2\xB3 \xB7 3. HCF(\u03B1, 24) = 1 means \u03B1 is divisible by neither 2 nor 3.
Step 2: Sum of 1 to 100 = 5050.
Step 3: Multiples of 2: 2(1 + 2 + ... + 50) = 2 \xB7 1275 = 2550. Multiples of 3: 3(1 + ... + 33) = 3 \xB7 561 = 1683. Multiples of 6 (counted twice): 6(1 + ... + 16) = 6 \xB7 136 = 816.
Step 4: Sum of numbers divisible by 2 or 3 = 2550 + 1683 \u2212 816 = 3417.
Step 5: Required sum = 5050 \u2212 3417 = 1633.
Answer: 1633`},{pn:40,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5Bn \u2208 \u2115 : H.C.F. (n, 45) = 1\uFF5D and let B = \uFF5B2k : k \u2208 \uFF5B1, 2, ..., 100\uFF5D\uFF5D. Then the sum of all the elements of A \u2229 B is ______.",n:5264,s:`Step 1: 45 = 3\xB2 \xB7 5, so n is in A when it is not divisible by 3 or 5. B is the even numbers 2, 4, ..., 200. So A \u2229 B is the even numbers up to 200 that are not multiples of 3 or 5.
Step 2: Sum of all of B = 2(1 + ... + 100) = 10100.
Step 3: Even multiples of 3 are multiples of 6 up to 198: 6(1 + ... + 33) = 6 \xB7 561 = 3366. Even multiples of 5 are multiples of 10 up to 200: 10(1 + ... + 20) = 2100. Even multiples of both (multiples of 30, up to 180): 30(1 + ... + 6) = 630.
Step 4: Removed sum = 3366 + 2100 \u2212 630 = 4836.
Step 5: Required sum = 10100 \u2212 4836 = 5264.
Answer: 5264`},{pn:41,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"If A = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x \u2212 2| > 1\uFF5D, B = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : \u221A(x\xB2 \u2212 3) > 1\uFF5D, C = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : |x \u2212 4| \u2265 2\uFF5D and \u2124 is the set of all integers, then the number of subsets of the set (A \u2229 B \u2229 C)\u1D9C \u2229 \u2124 is ______.",n:256,s:`Step 1: Solve A. |x \u2212 2| > 1 means x \u2212 2 > 1 or x \u2212 2 < \u22121, so A = (\u2212\u221E, 1) \u222A (3, \u221E).
Step 2: Solve B. \u221A(x\xB2 \u2212 3) > 1 means x\xB2 \u2212 3 > 1, so x\xB2 > 4, i.e. x < \u22122 or x > 2. B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A (2, \u221E).
Step 3: Find A \u2229 B. Left part: x < 1 and (x < \u22122 or x > 2) gives x < \u22122. Right part: x > 3 and (x < \u22122 or x > 2) gives x > 3. So A \u2229 B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A (3, \u221E).
Step 4: Solve C. |x \u2212 4| \u2265 2 means x \u2212 4 \u2265 2 or x \u2212 4 \u2264 \u22122, so C = (\u2212\u221E, 2] \u222A [6, \u221E).
Step 5: Find A \u2229 B \u2229 C. From (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A (3, \u221E) keep only the points in C: (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) stays, and in (3, \u221E) only [6, \u221E) is in C. So A \u2229 B \u2229 C = (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A [6, \u221E).
Step 6: Take the complement: (A \u2229 B \u2229 C)\u1D9C = [\u22122, 6).
Step 7: The integers in [\u22122, 6) are \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. That is 8 integers.
Step 8: A set with 8 elements has 2\u2078 = 256 subsets.
Answer: 256`},{pn:42,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"The sum of all the elements in the set \uFF5Bn \u2208 \uFF5B1, 2, ..., 100\uFF5D | H.C.F. of n and 2040 is 1\uFF5D is equal to ______.",n:1251,s:`Step 1: 2040 = 2\xB3 \xB7 3 \xB7 5 \xB7 17. So n must not be divisible by 2, 3, 5 or 17.
Step 2: First list numbers up to 100 that are not divisible by 2, 3, 5: 1, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 29 (sum 120); 31, 37, 41, 43, 47, 49, 53, 59 (sum 360); 61, 67, 71, 73, 77, 79, 83, 89 (sum 600); 91, 97 (sum 188).
Step 3: Their total = 120 + 360 + 600 + 188 = 1268.
Step 4: Now remove those divisible by 17. Among this list only 17 itself is (17 \xB7 7 = 119 is above 100).
Step 5: Required sum = 1268 \u2212 17 = 1251.
Answer: 1251`},{pn:43,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let A = \uFF5Bn \u2208 \u2115 : n is a 3-digit number\uFF5D, B = \uFF5B9k + 2 : k \u2208 \u2115\uFF5D and C = \uFF5B9k + l : k \u2208 \u2115\uFF5D for some l (0 < l < 9). If the sum of all the elements of the set A \u2229 (B \u222A C) is 274 \xD7 400, then l is equal to ______.",n:5,s:`Step 1: 3-digit numbers of the form 9k + 2: the smallest is 9\xB711 + 2 = 101 and the largest is 9\xB7110 + 2 = 992. That is k = 11 to 110, which is 100 terms.
Step 2: For any l from 1 to 8, 9k + l is 3-digit exactly for k = 11 to 110 too (9\xB711 + l \u2265 100 and 9\xB7110 + l \u2264 998). So C also gives 100 terms.
Step 3: If l \u2260 2, B and C leave different remainders on dividing by 9, so they do not overlap. The sum is \u03A3(9k + 2) + \u03A3(9k + l).
Step 4: \u03A3k for k = 11 to 110 is 100 \xB7 121/2 = 6050, so 9\u03A3k = 54450. Then B-sum = 54450 + 200 = 54650 and C-sum = 54450 + 100l.
Step 5: Total = 109100 + 100l = 274 \xD7 400 = 109600, so 100l = 500.
Step 6: l = 5 (and it is not 2, as assumed).
Answer: 5`},{pn:44,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Set A has m elements and Set B has n elements. If the total number of subsets of A is 112 more than the total number of subsets of B, then the value of m \xB7 n is ______.",n:28,s:`Step 1: A set with m elements has 2\u1D50 subsets. So 2\u1D50 \u2212 2\u207F = 112.
Step 2: Take 2\u207F common: 2\u207F(2\u1D50\u207B\u207F \u2212 1) = 112 = 16 \xD7 7.
Step 3: 2\u1D50\u207B\u207F \u2212 1 is odd, so all the factors of 2 in 112 belong to 2\u207F. 112 = 2\u2074 \xB7 7, so 2\u207F = 16 and n = 4.
Step 4: Then 2\u1D50\u207B\u207F \u2212 1 = 7, so 2\u1D50\u207B\u207F = 8 and m \u2212 n = 3, giving m = 7.
Step 5: m \xB7 n = 7 \xD7 4 = 28. Check: 128 \u2212 16 = 112.
Answer: 28`},{pn:45,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:"Let X = \uFF5Bn \u2208 \u2115 : 1 \u2264 n \u2264 50\uFF5D. If A = \uFF5Bn \u2208 X : n is a multiple of 2\uFF5D and B = \uFF5Bn \u2208 X : n is a multiple of 7\uFF5D, then the number of elements in the smallest subset of X containing both A and B is ______.",n:29,s:`Step 1: The smallest set containing both A and B is their union A \u222A B.
Step 2: A = \uFF5B2, 4, ..., 50\uFF5D, so n(A) = 25. B = \uFF5B7, 14, ..., 49\uFF5D, so n(B) = 7.
Step 3: A \u2229 B is the multiples of 14: 14, 28, 42, so n(A \u2229 B) = 3.
Step 4: n(A \u222A B) = n(A) + n(B) \u2212 n(A \u2229 B) = 25 + 7 \u2212 3 = 29.
Answer: 29`},{pn:46,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"sets",st:"Sets and their operations",q:`Let \u03A9 be the sample space and A \u2286 \u03A9 be an event. Given below are two statements
(S1): If P(A) = 0, then A = \u2205
(S2): If P(A) = 1, then A = \u03A9
Then`,o:["both (S1) and (S2) are true","only (S1) is true","only (S2) is true","both (S1) and (S2) are false"],c:3,s:`Note: the printed key says (a); working gives (d).
Step 1: An event with probability 0 need not be empty. Example: choose a point at random on a line segment [0, 1]; the event "the point is exactly 1/2" is not empty but has probability 0. So (S1) is false.
Step 2: Taking the complement of that event gives the event "the point is not 1/2", which has probability 1 but is not the whole sample space \u03A9. So (S2) is false.
Step 3: The same happens in a finite sample space if some outcome is given probability 0.
Step 4: Both statements are false in general.
Answer: (d)`},{pn:47,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"rel",st:"Types of relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, ..., 100\uFF5D and R be a relation on A such that R = \uFF5B(a, b) : a = 2b + 1\uFF5D. Let (a\u2081, a\u2082), (a\u2082, a\u2083), (a\u2083, a\u2084), ..., (a\u2096, a\u2096\u208A\u2081) be a sequence of k elements of R such that the second entry of an ordered pair is equal to the first entry of the next ordered pair. Then the largest integer k, for which such a sequence exists, is equal to :",o:["8","5","6","7"],c:1,s:`Step 1: (a, b) is in R when a = 2b + 1, so each first entry is 1 more than twice the second entry.
Step 2: In the sequence, a\u2081 = 2a\u2082 + 1, a\u2082 = 2a\u2083 + 1, ..., a\u2096 = 2a\u2096\u208A\u2081 + 1. So the sequence uses k + 1 numbers, going down from a\u2081 to a\u2096\u208A\u2081.
Step 3: The smallest possible last number is a\u2096\u208A\u2081 = 1. Then a\u2096 = 3, a\u2096\u208B\u2081 = 7, then 15, 31, 63, 127...
Step 4: All numbers must be in A (at most 100). The chain 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, 63 is fine, but the next number 127 is more than 100.
Step 5: So the largest chain has 6 numbers, which means 5 ordered pairs: (63, 31), (31, 15), (15, 7), (7, 3), (3, 1).
Answer: (b) 5`},{pn:48,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"rel",st:"Types of relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D \xD7 \u211D : |x + y| \u2265 3\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 \u211D \xD7 \u211D : |x| + |y| \u2264 3\uFF5D. If C = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 A \u2229 B : x = 0 or y = 0\uFF5D, then \u03A3(x, y) \u2208 C |x + y| is:",o:["18","12","24","15"],c:1,s:`Step 1: For any x, y we know |x + y| \u2264 |x| + |y|. A says |x + y| \u2265 3 and B says |x| + |y| \u2264 3.
Step 2: Together 3 \u2264 |x + y| \u2264 |x| + |y| \u2264 3, so everything must be equal to 3: |x + y| = 3 and |x| + |y| = 3.
Step 3: Now add the condition x = 0 or y = 0. If x = 0 then |y| = 3, so the points are (0, 3) and (0, \u22123). If y = 0 then |x| = 3, so the points are (3, 0) and (\u22123, 0).
Step 4: C has 4 points, and |x + y| = 3 for each.
Step 5: Sum = 4 \xD7 3 = 12.
Answer: (b) 12`},{pn:49,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let S = \u2115 \u222A \uFF5B0\uFF5D. Define a relation R from S to \u211D by: R = \uFF5B(x, y) : log\u2091 y = x log\u2091(2/5), x \u2208 S, y \u2208 \u211D\uFF5D. Then, the sum of all the elements in the range of R is equal to :",o:["5/2","5/3","3/2","10/9"],c:1,s:`Step 1: From log\u2091 y = x log\u2091(2/5) = log\u2091((2/5)\u02E3), we get y = (2/5)\u02E3.
Step 2: x takes the values 0, 1, 2, 3, ... so the range is \uFF5B1, 2/5, 4/25, 8/125, ...\uFF5D.
Step 3: Different x give different values (since 2/5 < 1), so the sum of the elements of the range is 1 + 2/5 + (2/5)\xB2 + ...
Step 4: This is an infinite geometric series with first term 1 and common ratio 2/5. Sum = 1/(1 \u2212 2/5) = 1/(3/5) = 5/3.
Answer: (b) 5/3`},{pn:50,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B2, 3, 4\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B8, 9, 12\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in the relation R = \uFF5B((a\u2081, b\u2081), (a\u2082, b\u2082)) \u2208 (A \xD7 B, A \xD7 B) : a\u2081 divides b\u2081 and a\u2082 divides b\u2082\uFF5D is",o:["24","12","36","18"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Look at the two conditions: a\u2081 divides b\u2081 and a\u2082 divides b\u2082. The two conditions are on separate pairs, so they can be counted separately.
Step 2: Count pairs (a, b) in A \xD7 B with a dividing b. 2 divides 8 and 12 (2 pairs); 3 divides 9 and 12 (2 pairs); 4 divides 8 and 12 (2 pairs). 2 does not divide 9, 3 does not divide 8, 4 does not divide 9.
Step 3: So there are 6 pairs (a, b) with a | b.
Step 4: (a\u2081, b\u2081) can be any of these 6 and (a\u2082, b\u2082) can independently be any of these 6.
Step 5: Number of elements in R = 6 \xD7 6 = 36.
Answer: (c) 36`},{pn:51,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 3, 4, 6, 9\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B2, 4, 5, 8, 10\uFF5D. Let R be a relation defined on A \xD7 B such that R = \uFF5B((a\u2081, b\u2081), (a\u2082, b\u2082)) : a\u2081 \u2264 b\u2082 and b\u2081 \u2264 a\u2082\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in the set R is",o:["52","180","26","160"],c:3,s:`Step 1: An element of R is a pair of pairs ((a\u2081, b\u2081), (a\u2082, b\u2082)) with a\u2081, a\u2082 from A and b\u2081, b\u2082 from B. The two conditions a\u2081 \u2264 b\u2082 and b\u2081 \u2264 a\u2082 involve different variables, so they can be counted separately and multiplied.
Step 2: Count pairs (a\u2081, b\u2082) from A \xD7 B with a\u2081 \u2264 b\u2082. For a\u2081 = 1: all 5 values of B; a\u2081 = 3: 4, 5, 8, 10 (4); a\u2081 = 4: 4, 5, 8, 10 (4); a\u2081 = 6: 8, 10 (2); a\u2081 = 9: 10 (1). Total = 5 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 1 = 16.
Step 3: Count pairs (b\u2081, a\u2082) from B \xD7 A with b\u2081 \u2264 a\u2082. For b\u2081 = 2: 3, 4, 6, 9 (4); b\u2081 = 4: 4, 6, 9 (3); b\u2081 = 5: 6, 9 (2); b\u2081 = 8: 9 (1); b\u2081 = 10: none (0). Total = 10.
Step 4: Each choice from Step 2 can be combined with each choice from Step 3, so the number of elements is 16 \xD7 10 = 160.
Answer: (d) 160`},{pn:52,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let R be a relation from the set \uFF5B1, 2, 3, ..., 60\uFF5D to itself such that R = \uFF5B(a, b) : b = pq, where p, q \u2265 3 are prime numbers\uFF5D. Then, the number of elements in R is:",o:["600","660","540","720"],c:1,s:`Step 1: First find all numbers b \u2264 60 of the form pq with p, q prime and both at least 3 (p and q may be equal).
Step 2: With p = 3: 3\xD73 = 9, 3\xD75 = 15, 3\xD77 = 21, 3\xD711 = 33, 3\xD713 = 39, 3\xD717 = 51, 3\xD719 = 57 (7 numbers).
Step 3: With p = 5: 5\xD75 = 25, 5\xD77 = 35, 5\xD711 = 55 (3 new numbers). With p = 7: 7\xD77 = 49 (1 new number). Next, 7\xD711 = 77 > 60, so we stop.
Step 4: So there are 7 + 3 + 1 = 11 possible values of b.
Step 5: The first element a can be any of the 60 numbers, with no condition on it. So the number of pairs is 60 \xD7 11 = 660.
Answer: (b) 660`},{pn:53,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let Z be the set of all integers, A = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 Z \xD7 Z : (x \u2212 2)\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2264 4\uFF5D, B = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 Z \xD7 Z : x\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2264 4\uFF5D and C = \uFF5B(x, y) \u2208 Z \xD7 Z : (x \u2212 2)\xB2 + (y \u2212 2)\xB2 \u2264 4\uFF5D. If the total number of relations from A \u2229 B to A \u2229 C is 2\u1D56, then the value of p is",o:["25","9","16","49"],c:0,s:`Step 1: A relation from a set P to a set Q is any subset of P \xD7 Q. If P has m elements and Q has n elements, there are 2^(mn) relations. So p = m \xD7 n.
Step 2: Find A \u2229 B: integer points inside both the circle centred (2, 0) and the circle centred (0, 0), each of radius 2. x = 0 gives only (0, 0); x = 1 gives (1, \u22121), (1, 0), (1, 1); x = 2 gives only (2, 0). So A \u2229 B has 5 points.
Step 3: Find A \u2229 C: points inside the circles centred (2, 0) and (2, 2). Put u = x \u2212 2: we need u\xB2 + y\xB2 \u2264 4 and u\xB2 + (y \u2212 2)\xB2 \u2264 4. y = 1 gives u = \u22121, 0, 1; y = 0 gives u = 0; y = 2 gives u = 0. So A \u2229 C also has 5 points.
Step 4: So p = 5 \xD7 5 = 25.
Answer: (a) 25`},{pn:54,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Types of relations",q:"If R = \uFF5B(x, y) : x, y \u2208 Z, x\xB2 + 3y\xB2 \u2264 8\uFF5D is a relation on the set of integers Z, then the domain of R\u207B\xB9 is",o:["\uFF5B\u22122, \u22121, 1, 2\uFF5D","\uFF5B0, 1\uFF5D","\uFF5B\u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2\uFF5D","\uFF5B\u22121, 0, 1\uFF5D"],c:3,s:`Step 1: R\u207B\xB9 is got by swapping the pairs of R, so the domain of R\u207B\xB9 is the set of all second elements y that occur in R (the range of R).
Step 2: We need integers y for which some integer x satisfies x\xB2 + 3y\xB2 \u2264 8, i.e. x\xB2 \u2264 8 \u2212 3y\xB2.
Step 3: y = 0: x\xB2 \u2264 8 is possible (for example x = 0). y = 1 or \u22121: x\xB2 \u2264 5 is possible. y = 2 or \u22122: x\xB2 \u2264 8 \u2212 12 < 0, impossible. Larger |y| is also impossible.
Step 4: So the possible y values are \u22121, 0, 1.
Answer: (d) \uFF5B\u22121, 0, 1\uFF5D`},{pn:55,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let Z be the set of integers. If A = \uFF5Bx \u2208 Z : 2^((x+2)(x\xB2\u22125x+6)) = 1\uFF5D and B = \uFF5Bx \u2208 Z : \u22123 < 2x \u2212 1 < 9\uFF5D, then the number of subsets of the set A \xD7 B is",o:["2\xB9\u2078","2\xB9\xB2","2\xB9\u2075","2\xB9\u2070"],c:2,s:`Step 1: 2 raised to a power equals 1 only when the power is 0. So (x + 2)(x\xB2 \u2212 5x + 6) = 0.
Step 2: Factorise x\xB2 \u2212 5x + 6 = (x \u2212 2)(x \u2212 3). So (x + 2)(x \u2212 2)(x \u2212 3) = 0 and x = \u22122, 2, 3. Hence A has 3 elements.
Step 3: For B: \u22123 < 2x \u2212 1 < 9 gives \u22122 < 2x < 10, so \u22121 < x < 5. The integers are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, so B has 5 elements.
Step 4: A \xD7 B has 3 \xD7 5 = 15 elements.
Step 5: A set with n elements has 2\u207F subsets, so the number of subsets is 2\xB9\u2075.
Answer: (c) 2\xB9\u2075`},{pn:56,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let A and B be two sets containing 2 elements and 4 elements respectively. The number of subsets of A \xD7 B having 3 or more elements is",o:["2\xB9\xB9","256","220","219"],c:3,s:`Step 1: A \xD7 B has 2 \xD7 4 = 8 elements, so it has 2\u2078 = 256 subsets in all.
Step 2: Subsets with fewer than 3 elements: 0 elements: \u2078C\u2080 = 1; 1 element: \u2078C\u2081 = 8; 2 elements: \u2078C\u2082 = 28.
Step 3: That is 1 + 8 + 28 = 37 subsets to remove.
Step 4: Required number = 256 \u2212 37 = 219.
Answer: (d) 219`},{pn:57,y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let X = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5\uFF5D. The number of different ordered pairs (Y, Z) that can be formed such that Y \u2286 X, Z \u2286 X and Y \u2229 Z is empty is",o:["2\u2075","5\xB3","5\xB2","3\u2075"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Build Y and Z by looking at each element of X one at a time.
Step 2: Because Y \u2229 Z is empty, an element cannot be in both Y and Z. So each element has exactly 3 choices: only in Y, only in Z, or in neither.
Step 3: The 5 elements are decided independently, so the number of ordered pairs is 3 \xD7 3 \xD7 3 \xD7 3 \xD7 3 = 3\u2075 = 243.
Answer: (d) 3\u2075`},{pn:58,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"sets",st:"Power set and subsets",q:"Let S = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D. The total number of unordered pairs of disjoint subsets of S is equal to",o:["25","34","42","41"],c:3,s:`Step 1: First count ordered pairs (A, B) of disjoint subsets. Each element of S goes into A only, B only, or neither: 3 choices each, so 3\u2074 = 81 ordered pairs.
Step 2: In an unordered pair \uFF5BA, B\uFF5D, (A, B) and (B, A) are the same. The two coincide only when A = B; as they are disjoint, that means A = B = \u2205. That is 1 ordered pair.
Step 3: The other 81 \u2212 1 = 80 ordered pairs group in twos, giving 40 unordered pairs.
Step 4: Add the single pair \uFF5B\u2205, \u2205\uFF5D: total = 40 + 1 = 41.
Answer: (d) 41`},{pn:59,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B2, 3, 6, 7\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B4, 5, 6, 8\uFF5D. Let R be a relation defined on A \xD7 B by (a\u2081, b\u2081) R (a\u2082, b\u2082) if and only if a\u2081 + a\u2082 = b\u2081 + b\u2082. Then the number of elements in R is",n:25,s:`Step 1: We need ordered choices (a\u2081, a\u2082) from A and (b\u2081, b\u2082) from B with the same sum. List how many ways each sum arises in each set (16 ordered pairs each).
Step 2: Sums from A: 4 (1 way), 5 (2), 6 (1), 8 (2), 9 (4), 10 (2), 12 (1), 13 (2), 14 (1).
Step 3: Sums from B: 8 (1), 9 (2), 10 (3), 11 (2), 12 (3), 13 (2), 14 (2), 16 (1).
Step 4: Common sums, multiplying the ways: 8: 2\xD71 = 2; 9: 4\xD72 = 8; 10: 2\xD73 = 6; 12: 1\xD73 = 3; 13: 2\xD72 = 4; 14: 1\xD72 = 2.
Step 5: Total = 2 + 8 + 6 + 3 + 4 + 2 = 25 (checked by a computer count as well).
Answer: 25`},{pn:60,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, ..., 20\uFF5D. Let R\u2081 and R\u2082 be two relations on A such that R\u2081 = \uFF5B(a, b) : b is divisible by a\uFF5D and R\u2082 = \uFF5B(a, b) : a is an integral multiple of b\uFF5D. Then, the number of elements in R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082 is equal to",n:46,s:`Step 1: (a, b) is in R\u2081 when a divides b, and in R\u2082 when b divides a. A pair is in both exactly when a divides b and b divides a, i.e. a = b.
Step 2: So R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082 = R\u2081 without the 20 pairs (a, a). Hence |R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082| = |R\u2081| \u2212 20.
Step 3: For each a, the number of b in 1..20 divisible by a is \u230A20/a\u230B. Add this up over a = 1 to 20.
Step 4: For a = 1..10 the values are 20, 10, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 2, 2, 2 (sum 56); for a = 11..20 each is 1 (sum 10). So |R\u2081| = 66.
Step 5: |R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082| = 66 \u2212 20 = 46.
Answer: 46`},{pn:61,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, ..., 10\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B0, 1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D. The number of elements in the relation R = \uFF5B(a, b) \u2208 A \xD7 A : 2(a \u2212 b)\xB2 + 3(a \u2212 b) \u2208 B\uFF5D is",n:18,s:`Step 1: Let d = a \u2212 b. We need 2d\xB2 + 3d to be one of 0, 1, 2, 3, 4.
Step 2: Try integer values of d: d = 0 gives 0 (ok); d = 1 gives 5 (no); d = \u22121 gives \u22121 (no); d = \u22122 gives 8 \u2212 6 = 2 (ok); d = \u22123 gives 18 \u2212 9 = 9 (no); d = 2 gives 14 (no). Larger |d| gives bigger values, so no more.
Step 3: So d = 0 or d = \u22122, i.e. a = b or b = a + 2.
Step 4: a = b within 1..10 gives 10 pairs. b = a + 2 needs a from 1 to 8, giving 8 pairs.
Step 5: Total = 10 + 8 = 18.
Answer: 18`},{pn:62,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D and R be a relation on the set A \xD7 A defined by R = \uFF5B((a, b), (c, d)) : 2a + 3b = 4c + 5d\uFF5D. Then the number of elements in R is",n:6,s:`Step 1: For a, b in 1..4, the value 2a + 3b ranges from 5 to 20. So we only need (c, d) with 4c + 5d \u2264 20.
Step 2: Those (c, d) are: (1,1) gives 9, (2,1) gives 13, (1,2) gives 14, (3,1) gives 17, (2,2) gives 18, (1,3) gives 19.
Step 3: Count the (a, b) with 2a + 3b equal to each value: 9: (3,1) \u2192 1; 13: (2,3) \u2192 1; 14: (4,2) and (1,4) \u2192 2; 17: (4,3) \u2192 1; 18: (3,4) \u2192 1; 19: none \u2192 0.
Step 4: Total = 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 + 1 + 0 = 6.
Answer: 6`},{pn:63,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"rel",st:"Counting relations",q:"Let R\u2081 and R\u2082 be relations on the set \uFF5B1, 2, ..., 50\uFF5D such that R\u2081 = \uFF5B(p, p\u207F) : p is a prime and n \u2265 0 is an integer\uFF5D and R\u2082 = \uFF5B(p, p\u207F) : p is a prime and n = 0 or 1\uFF5D. Then, the number of elements in R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082 is",n:8,s:`Step 1: The pairs in R\u2081 are (p, p\u207F) with p\u207F \u2264 50 (the second entry must lie in the set). R\u2082 keeps only n = 0 or 1.
Step 2: So R\u2081 \u2212 R\u2082 consists of the pairs (p, p\u207F) with n \u2265 2 and p\u207F \u2264 50. Different (p, n) give different pairs.
Step 3: p = 2: 4, 8, 16, 32 (4 pairs). p = 3: 9, 27 (2 pairs). p = 5: 25 (1 pair). p = 7: 49 (1 pair). p = 11: 121 is too big.
Step 4: Total = 4 + 2 + 1 + 1 = 8.
Answer: 8`},{pn:64,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the domain of the function f(x) = 1/\u221A(10 + 3x \u2212 x\xB2) + 1/\u221A(x + |x|) is (a, b), then (1 + a)\xB2 + b\xB2 is equal to :",o:["29","26","30","25"],c:1,s:`Step 1: For the first term the expression under the root must be positive: 10 + 3x \u2212 x\xB2 > 0, i.e. x\xB2 \u2212 3x \u2212 10 < 0.
Step 2: Factorise: (x \u2212 5)(x + 2) < 0, so \u22122 < x < 5.
Step 3: For the second term x + |x| > 0. If x \u2264 0 then x + |x| = 0, which fails. If x > 0 then x + |x| = 2x > 0, which is fine. So x > 0.
Step 4: Both conditions hold for 0 < x < 5, so the domain is (0, 5): a = 0, b = 5.
Step 5: (1 + a)\xB2 + b\xB2 = 1 + 25 = 26.
Answer: (b) 26`},{pn:65,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the domain of the function f(x) = log\u2087(1 \u2212 log\u2084(x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 18)) is (\u03B1, \u03B2) \u222A (\u03B3, \u03B4), then \u03B1 + \u03B2 + \u03B3 + \u03B4 is equal to",o:["18","15","16","17"],c:0,s:`Step 1: The inner log needs x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 18 > 0, i.e. (x \u2212 3)(x \u2212 6) > 0, so x < 3 or x > 6.
Step 2: The outer log needs 1 \u2212 log\u2084(x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 18) > 0, i.e. log\u2084(x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 18) < 1.
Step 3: Since the base 4 is greater than 1, this means x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 18 < 4, i.e. x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 14 < 0, i.e. (x \u2212 2)(x \u2212 7) < 0, so 2 < x < 7.
Step 4: Combine: (2 < x < 7) with (x < 3 or x > 6) gives (2, 3) \u222A (6, 7).
Step 5: \u03B1 + \u03B2 + \u03B3 + \u03B4 = 2 + 3 + 6 + 7 = 18.
Answer: (a) 18`},{pn:66,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f be a function such that f(x) + 3f(24/x) = 4x, x \u2260 0. Then f(3) + f(8) is equal to",o:["13","12","10","11"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Given f(x) + 3f(24/x) = 4x. (1)
Step 2: Replace x by 24/x. Then 24/x becomes x, giving f(24/x) + 3f(x) = 96/x. (2)
Step 3: Multiply (2) by 3: 3f(24/x) + 9f(x) = 288/x. Subtract (1): 8f(x) = 288/x \u2212 4x.
Step 4: So f(x) = 36/x \u2212 x/2.
Step 5: f(3) = 12 \u2212 3/2 = 21/2 and f(8) = 36/8 \u2212 4 = 1/2.
Step 6: f(3) + f(8) = 21/2 + 1/2 = 11.
Answer: (d) 11`},{pn:67,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the range of the function f(x) = (5 \u2212 x)/(x\xB2 \u2212 3x + 2), x \u2260 1, 2, is (\u2212\u221E, \u03B1] \u222A [\u03B2, \u221E), then \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 is equal to :",o:["190","194","188","192"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Put y = (5 \u2212 x)/(x\xB2 \u2212 3x + 2). Then y(x\xB2 \u2212 3x + 2) = 5 \u2212 x, i.e. yx\xB2 + (1 \u2212 3y)x + (2y \u2212 5) = 0.
Step 2: For a given y to be in the range, this equation must have a real root x (the values x = 1, 2 give a nonzero numerator 4 and 3 in the equation, so they are never a root and need no exclusion). So the discriminant must be \u2265 0.
Step 3: D = (1 \u2212 3y)\xB2 \u2212 4y(2y \u2212 5) = 1 \u2212 6y + 9y\xB2 \u2212 8y\xB2 + 20y = y\xB2 + 14y + 1 \u2265 0.
Step 4: Roots of y\xB2 + 14y + 1 = 0 are y = \u22127 \xB1 4\u221A3. So y \u2264 \u22127 \u2212 4\u221A3 or y \u2265 \u22127 + 4\u221A3 (y = 0 is covered, with x = 5).
Step 5: \u03B1 = \u22127 \u2212 4\u221A3 and \u03B2 = \u22127 + 4\u221A3. Then \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 = 2(49 + 48) = 194.
Answer: (b) 194`},{pn:68,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) = (2^(x+2) + 16)/(2^(2x+1) + 2^(x+4) + 32). Then the value of 8(f(1/15) + f(2/15) + ... + f(59/15)) is equal to",o:["118","102","92","108"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Put t = 2\u02E3. Numerator = 4t + 16 = 4(t + 4). Denominator = 2t\xB2 + 16t + 32 = 2(t + 4)\xB2.
Step 2: So f(x) = 4(t + 4) / (2(t + 4)\xB2) = 2/(t + 4) = 2/(2\u02E3 + 4).
Step 3: Check the sum f(x) + f(4 \u2212 x). With 2^(4\u2212x) = 16/t: f(4 \u2212 x) = 2/(16/t + 4) = t/(2(t + 4)). Then f(x) + f(4 \u2212 x) = 2/(t + 4) + t/(2(t + 4)) = (4 + t)/(2(t + 4)) = 1/2.
Step 4: The arguments are k/15 for k = 1, ..., 59. Pair k with 60 \u2212 k (their sum is 60/15 = 4). That gives 29 pairs (k = 1..29), each adding 1/2, and the middle term k = 30, i.e. f(2).
Step 5: f(2) = 2/(4 + 4) = 1/4. Sum = 29 \xD7 1/2 + 1/4 = 59/4.
Step 6: 8 \xD7 59/4 = 118.
Answer: (a) 118`},{pn:69,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f : R \u2192 R be a function defined by f(x) = (2 + 3a)x\xB2 + ((a + 2)/(a \u2212 1))x + b, a \u2260 1. If f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) + 1 \u2212 (2/7)xy, then the value of 28 \u03A3 (i = 1 to 5) |f(i)| is",o:["675","545","735","715"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Put x = y = 0: f(0) = 2f(0) + 1, so f(0) = \u22121. But f(0) = b, so b = \u22121.
Step 2: Compare the xy terms. Left side f(x + y) has (2 + 3a)(x + y)\xB2, whose cross term is 2(2 + 3a)xy; the linear parts cancel. So 2(2 + 3a) = \u22122/7, giving 2 + 3a = \u22121/7 and a = \u22125/7.
Step 3: Then (a + 2)/(a \u2212 1) = (9/7)/(\u221212/7) = \u22123/4. So f(x) = \u2212x\xB2/7 \u2212 3x/4 \u2212 1.
Step 4: f(1) = \u221253/28, f(2) = \u221286/28, f(3) = \u2212127/28, f(4) = \u2212176/28, f(5) = \u2212233/28 (all negative, so |f(i)| is the positive value).
Step 5: \u03A3|f(i)| = (53 + 86 + 127 + 176 + 233)/28 = 675/28.
Step 6: 28 \xD7 675/28 = 675.
Answer: (a) 675`},{pn:70,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The sum of all local minimum values of the function f(x) = 1 \u2212 2x for x < \u22121; (1/3)(7 + 2|x|) for \u22121 \u2264 x \u2264 2; (11/18)(x \u2212 4)(x \u2212 5) for x > 2, is",o:["131/72","171/72","167/72","157/72"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Study each piece. For x < \u22121, f = 1 \u2212 2x is decreasing, and it reaches 3 at x = \u22121. Also, at x = \u22121 the middle piece gives (7 + 2)/3 = 3, so f is continuous there.
Step 2: For \u22121 \u2264 x \u2264 2, f = (7 + 2|x|)/3. It decreases from 3 to 7/3 on [\u22121, 0] and increases from 7/3 to 11/3 on [0, 2]. So x = 0 is a local minimum with value 7/3. (x = \u22121 is not a minimum, f keeps decreasing across it.)
Step 3: At x = 2 the right piece gives (11/18)(\u22122)(\u22123) = 11/3, so f is continuous. On the right, (11/18)(x\xB2 \u2212 9x + 20) decreases up to x = 9/2. So f rises up to x = 2, then falls: x = 2 is a local maximum, not a minimum.
Step 4: The parabola piece has its minimum at x = 9/2 (inside x > 2): f = (11/18)(1/2)(\u22121/2) = \u221211/72. So x = 9/2 is a local minimum.
Step 5: Sum of local minimum values = 7/3 \u2212 11/72 = 168/72 \u2212 11/72 = 157/72.
Answer: (d) 157/72`},{pn:71,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x) = 2\u02E3/(2\u02E3 + \u221A2), x \u2208 R, then \u03A3 (k = 1 to 81) f(k/82) is equal to",o:["81/2","82","41","81\u221A2"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Find f(x) + f(1 \u2212 x). With t = 2\u02E3, 2^(1\u2212x) = 2/t.
Step 2: f(1 \u2212 x) = (2/t)/((2/t) + \u221A2) = 2/(2 + \u221A2 t) = \u221A2/(\u221A2 + t).
Step 3: So f(x) + f(1 \u2212 x) = t/(t + \u221A2) + \u221A2/(t + \u221A2) = 1.
Step 4: In the sum, pair k/82 with (82 \u2212 k)/82 (their sum is 1). For k = 1 to 40 these pair with k = 81 to 42: 40 pairs, each giving 1, i.e. 40.
Step 5: The unpaired middle term is k = 41: f(1/2) = \u221A2/(\u221A2 + \u221A2) = 1/2.
Step 6: Sum = 40 + 1/2 = 81/2.
Answer: (a) 81/2`},{pn:72,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the domain of the function log\u2085(18x \u2212 x\xB2 \u2212 77) is (\u03B1, \u03B2) and the domain of the function log_(x\u22121)((2x\xB2 + 3x \u2212 2)/(x\xB2 \u2212 3x \u2212 4)) is (\u03B3, \u03B4), then \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 + \u03B3\xB2 is equal to :",o:["195","174","186","179"],c:2,s:`Step 1: First function: 18x \u2212 x\xB2 \u2212 77 > 0, i.e. x\xB2 \u2212 18x + 77 < 0, i.e. (x \u2212 7)(x \u2212 11) < 0. So the domain is (7, 11): \u03B1 = 7, \u03B2 = 11.
Step 2: Second function: the base must be positive and not 1, so x \u2212 1 > 0 and x \u2260 2, i.e. x > 1, x \u2260 2.
Step 3: The argument must be positive: (2x \u2212 1)(x + 2)/((x \u2212 4)(x + 1)) > 0. The critical points are \u22122, \u22121, 1/2, 4. The expression is positive for x > 4, negative on (1/2, 4), positive on (\u22121, 1/2), negative on (\u22122, \u22121), positive for x < \u22122.
Step 4: Keeping x > 1 leaves only x > 4. So the domain is (4, \u221E): \u03B3 = 4 (\u03B4 is infinity and is not needed).
Step 5: \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 + \u03B3\xB2 = 49 + 121 + 16 = 186.
Answer: (c) 186`},{pn:73,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let f(x) = 1/(7 \u2212 sin 5x) be a function defined on R. Then the range of the function f(x) is equal to :",o:["[1/7, 1/5]","[1/7, 1/6]","[1/8, 1/6]","[1/8, 1/5]"],c:2,s:`Step 1: sin 5x takes every value between \u22121 and 1.
Step 2: So 7 \u2212 sin 5x takes values from 7 \u2212 1 = 6 to 7 + 1 = 8, i.e. it lies in [6, 8].
Step 3: The reciprocal of a positive number decreases as the number increases, so 1/(7 \u2212 sin 5x) goes from 1/8 (when the denominator is 8) to 1/6 (when it is 6).
Step 4: Every value in between is reached, so the range is [1/8, 1/6].
Answer: (c) [1/8, 1/6]`},{pn:74,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the domain of the function f(x) = \u221A(x\xB2 \u2212 25)/(4 \u2212 x\xB2) + log\u2081\u2080(x\xB2 + 2x \u2212 15) is (\u2212\u221E, \u03B1) \u222A [\u03B2, \u221E), then \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB3 is equal to:",o:["140","175","125","150"],c:3,s:`Step 1: The square root needs x\xB2 \u2212 25 \u2265 0, so x \u2264 \u22125 or x \u2265 5.
Step 2: The denominator needs 4 \u2212 x\xB2 \u2260 0, i.e. x \u2260 \xB12. These values are already outside the set from Step 1, so nothing is lost.
Step 3: The log needs x\xB2 + 2x \u2212 15 > 0, i.e. (x + 5)(x \u2212 3) > 0, so x < \u22125 or x > 3.
Step 4: Intersect: x < \u22125 (here x = \u22125 is dropped by the log) or x \u2265 5. So the domain is (\u2212\u221E, \u22125) \u222A [5, \u221E): \u03B1 = \u22125, \u03B2 = 5.
Step 5: \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB3 = 25 + 125 = 150.
Answer: (d) 150`},{pn:75,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"Let A = \uFF5Bx \u2208 R : [x + 3] + [x + 4] \u2264 3\uFF5D, B = \uFF5Bx \u2208 R : 3\u02E3 (\u03A3 (r = 1 to \u221E) 3/10\u02B3)^(x\u22123) < 3^(\u22123x)\uFF5D, where [t] denotes the greatest integer function. Then,",o:["A \u2229 B = \u2205","B \u2282 C, A \u2260 B","A \u2282 B, A \u2260 B","A = B"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Set A. [x + 3] = [x] + 3 and [x + 4] = [x] + 4, so the condition is 2[x] + 7 \u2264 3, i.e. [x] \u2264 \u22122. That means x < \u22121. So A = (\u2212\u221E, \u22121).
Step 2: Set B. The infinite sum \u03A3 3/10\u02B3 = 3 \xD7 (1/10)/(1 \u2212 1/10) = 1/3 (a geometric series).
Step 3: So the condition is 3\u02E3 (1/3)^(x\u22123) < 3^(\u22123x). Now (1/3)^(x\u22123) = 3^(3\u2212x), so the left side is 3\u02E3 \xB7 3^(3\u2212x) = 3\xB3 = 27.
Step 4: We need 27 < 3^(\u22123x), i.e. 3\xB3 < 3^(\u22123x), so \u22123x > 3 and x < \u22121. So B = (\u2212\u221E, \u22121).
Step 5: A and B are the same set. (The crop prints option (b) as "B \u2282 C, A \u2260 B"; C is not defined, but only (d) fits anyway.)
Answer: (d) A = B`},{pn:76,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The number of integral solutions x of log_(x+7/2) ((x\u22127)/(2x\u22123))\xB2 \u2265 0 is",o:["6","8","5","7"],c:0,s:`Step 1: A logarithm needs a positive base that is not 1: x + 7/2 > 0 and x + 7/2 \u2260 1, so x > \u22127/2 and x \u2260 \u22125/2.
Step 2: The argument ((x\u22127)/(2x\u22123))\xB2 must be positive, so x \u2260 7 and x \u2260 3/2.
Step 3: Case base > 1 (x > \u22125/2): log \u2265 0 means argument \u2265 1, i.e. (x\u22127)\xB2 \u2265 (2x\u22123)\xB2. This gives (x+4)(3x\u221210) \u2264 0, so \u22124 \u2264 x \u2264 10/3.
Step 4: Combine with x > \u22125/2 and x \u2260 3/2: x \u2208 (\u22125/2, 10/3] except 3/2. Integers: \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, 3 (that is 6).
Step 5: Case 0 < base < 1 (\u22127/2 < x < \u22125/2): log \u2265 0 means argument \u2264 1, i.e. (x+4)(3x\u221210) \u2265 0, so x \u2264 \u22124 or x \u2265 10/3. Nothing lies in (\u22127/2, \u22125/2), so no solutions here.
Answer: 6, option (a).`},{pn:77,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"The domain of the function f(x) = 1/\u221A([x]\xB2 \u2212 3[x] \u2212 10) is (where [x] denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to x)",o:["(\u2212\u221E, \u22123] \u222A (5, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A [6, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u22123] \u222A [6, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A (5, \u221E)"],c:1,s:`Step 1: The root is in the denominator, so we need [x]\xB2 \u2212 3[x] \u2212 10 > 0 (strictly positive).
Step 2: Factorise: ([x] \u2212 5)([x] + 2) > 0.
Step 3: This holds when [x] > 5 or [x] < \u22122. Since [x] is an integer, [x] \u2265 6 or [x] \u2264 \u22123.
Step 4: [x] \u2265 6 means x \u2265 6. [x] \u2264 \u22123 means x < \u22122 (for example x = \u22122.5 has [x] = \u22123, but x = \u22122 has [x] = \u22122, which is not allowed).
Answer: (\u2212\u221E, \u22122) \u222A [6, \u221E), option (b).`},{pn:78,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2212 \uFF5B0, 1\uFF5D \u2192 \u211D be a function such that f(x) + f(1/(1\u2212x)) = 1 + x. Then f(2) is equal to",o:["7/3","9/2","9/4","7/4"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Put x = 2. Then 1/(1\u2212x) = \u22121, so f(2) + f(\u22121) = 3.   ... (i)
Step 2: Put x = \u22121. Then 1/(1\u2212x) = 1/2, so f(\u22121) + f(1/2) = 0.   ... (ii)
Step 3: Put x = 1/2. Then 1/(1\u2212x) = 2, so f(1/2) + f(2) = 3/2.   ... (iii)
Step 4: Add (i), (ii), (iii): 2[f(2) + f(\u22121) + f(1/2)] = 3 + 0 + 3/2 = 9/2, so the three values add up to 9/4.
Step 5: By (ii), f(\u22121) + f(1/2) = 0, so f(2) = 9/4 \u2212 0.
Answer: 9/4, option (c).`},{pn:79,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"The equation x\xB2 \u2212 4x + [x] + 3 = x[x], where [x] denotes the greatest integer function, has",o:["no solution","exactly two solutions in (\u2212\u221E, \u221E)","a unique solution in (\u2212\u221E, 1)","a unique solution in (\u2212\u221E, \u221E)"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Write x = n + t with n = [x] an integer and 0 \u2264 t < 1.
Step 2: Substitute: (n+t)\xB2 \u2212 4(n+t) + n + 3 = n(n+t). Cancel n\xB2 and simplify: t\xB2 + (n \u2212 4)t \u2212 3(n \u2212 1) = 0. Call the left side g(t).
Step 3: Evaluate at the ends: g(0) = 3 \u2212 3n and g(1) = \u22122n.
Step 4: If n \u2265 2, both g(0) and g(1) are negative and the parabola opens upward, so g stays negative on [0, 1): no root. If n \u2264 0, the vertex is at t = (4 \u2212 n)/2 > 2, so g is decreasing on [0, 1] and stays positive for t < 1 (g(1) = 0 only when n = 0, but t = 1 is not allowed): no root.
Step 5: If n = 1: g(t) = t\xB2 \u2212 3t = 0, so t = 0 (t = 3 is rejected). Thus x = 1. Check: 1 \u2212 4 + 1 + 3 = 1 = 1\xB71.
Step 6: So the only solution is x = 1, which is not less than 1, but it is the unique solution on the whole real line.
Answer: option (d).`},{pn:80,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x) = 2^(2x)/(2^(2x) + 2), x \u2208 \u211D, then f(1/2023) + f(2/2023) + ... + f(2022/2023) is equal to",o:["1011","2010","1010","2011"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Write f(x) = 4^x/(4^x + 2).
Step 2: Find f(1 \u2212 x) = 4^(1\u2212x)/(4^(1\u2212x) + 2) = 4/(4 + 2\xB74^x) = 2/(4^x + 2).
Step 3: Add: f(x) + f(1 \u2212 x) = (4^x + 2)/(4^x + 2) = 1.
Step 4: In the sum, the terms k/2023 and (2023 \u2212 k)/2023 add up to 1 in argument, so each such pair contributes 1.
Step 5: There are 2022 terms, which make 1011 pairs, each giving 1.
Answer: 1011, option (a).`},{pn:81,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) be a function such that f(x + y) = f(x)\xB7f(y) for all x, y \u2208 \u2115. If f(1) = 3 and the sum of f(k) for k = 1 to n equals 3279, then the value of n is",o:["8","6","7","9"],c:2,s:`Step 1: f(2) = f(1)\xB7f(1) = 9, f(3) = f(2)\xB7f(1) = 27, so f(k) = 3^k.
Step 2: The sum 3 + 3\xB2 + ... + 3^n is a geometric series with sum 3(3^n \u2212 1)/2.
Step 3: Set 3(3^n \u2212 1)/2 = 3279, so 3^n \u2212 1 = 2186 and 3^n = 2187.
Step 4: 2187 = 3^7 (3^7 = 3\xB73\xB73\xB73\xB73\xB73\xB73).
Answer: n = 7, option (c).`},{pn:82,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Counting functions",q:"The number of functions f: \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D \u2192 \uFF5Ba \u2208 \u2124 : |a| \u2264 8\uFF5D satisfying f(n) + (1/n)\xB7f(n + 1) = 1, for all n \u2208 \uFF5B1, 2, 3\uFF5D is",o:["2","1","4","3"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Rewrite the rule as f(n+1) = n(1 \u2212 f(n)). So once f(1) = a is chosen, everything else is fixed.
Step 2: f(2) = 1 \u2212 a, f(3) = 2(1 \u2212 f(2)) = 2a, f(4) = 3(1 \u2212 f(3)) = 3 \u2212 6a.
Step 3: All four values must be integers between \u22128 and 8. |a| \u2264 8, |1 \u2212 a| \u2264 8, |2a| \u2264 8 gives |a| \u2264 4, and |3 \u2212 6a| \u2264 8 gives \u22125/6 \u2264 a \u2264 11/6.
Step 4: The integers satisfying all of these are a = 0 and a = 1.
Step 5: Check: a = 0 gives (0, 1, 0, 3); a = 1 gives (1, 0, 2, \u22123). Both are valid. A brute-force count also gave 2.
Answer: 2, option (a). (The PDF gives no key for this question.)`},{pn:83,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) = 2x\u207F + \u03BB, \u03BB \u2208 \u211D, n \u2208 \u2115, and f(4) = 133, f(5) = 255. Then the sum of all the positive integer divisors of (f(3) \u2212 f(2)) is",o:["59","60","61","58"],c:1,s:`Step 1: f(4) = 2\xB74\u207F + \u03BB = 133 and f(5) = 2\xB75\u207F + \u03BB = 255. Subtract: 2(5\u207F \u2212 4\u207F) = 122, so 5\u207F \u2212 4\u207F = 61.
Step 2: Try small n: n = 3 gives 125 \u2212 64 = 61. So n = 3.
Step 3: From f(4): 2\xB764 + \u03BB = 133, so \u03BB = 5. Check f(5) = 250 + 5 = 255.
Step 4: f(3) = 2\xB727 + 5 = 59 and f(2) = 2\xB78 + 5 = 21, so f(3) \u2212 f(2) = 38.
Step 5: 38 = 2 \xD7 19, so its divisors are 1, 2, 19, 38.
Step 6: Their sum is 1 + 2 + 19 + 38 = 60.
Answer: 60, option (b).`},{pn:84,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be a function defined by f(x) = log_(\u221Am) \uFF5B\u221A2 (sin x \u2212 cos x) + m \u2212 2\uFF5D, for some m, such that the range of f is [0, 2]. Then the value of m is",o:["3","5","4","2"],c:1,s:`Step 1: sin x \u2212 cos x = \u221A2 sin(x \u2212 \u03C0/4), so it ranges over [\u2212\u221A2, \u221A2].
Step 2: Then \u221A2(sin x \u2212 cos x) ranges over [\u22122, 2], and the expression inside the log ranges over [m \u2212 4, m].
Step 3: The base \u221Am is greater than 1 (m > 1), so the log is increasing: the range is [log_(\u221Am)(m \u2212 4), log_(\u221Am)(m)].
Step 4: log_(\u221Am)(m) = 2 automatically, which matches the upper end 2.
Step 5: The lower end must be 0: log_(\u221Am)(m \u2212 4) = 0 means m \u2212 4 = 1, so m = 5.
Answer: m = 5, option (b).`},{pn:85,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of f(x) = log_(x+1)(x \u2212 2) / (e^(2 log_e x) \u2212 (2x + 3)), x \u2208 \u211D is",o:["(2, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B3\uFF5D","\u211D \u2212 \uFF5B3\uFF5D","\u211D \u2212 \uFF5B\u22121, 3\uFF5D","(\u22121, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B3\uFF5D"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Numerator log_(x+1)(x \u2212 2): the base needs x + 1 > 0 and x + 1 \u2260 1, and the argument needs x \u2212 2 > 0. Together these give x > 2.
Step 2: In the denominator, e^(2 log_e x) is defined only for x > 0 and equals x\xB2. So the denominator is x\xB2 \u2212 2x \u2212 3 = (x \u2212 3)(x + 1).
Step 3: The denominator must not be 0, so x \u2260 3 and x \u2260 \u22121.
Step 4: Combine with x > 2: x \u2208 (2, \u221E) except 3.
Answer: (2, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B3\uFF5D, option (a).`},{pn:86,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Consider a function f: \u2115 \u2192 \u211D, satisfying f(1) + 2f(2) + 3f(3) + ... + xf(x) = x(x + 1)f(x); x \u2265 2 with f(1) = 1. Then 1/f(2022) + 1/f(2028) is equal to",o:["8400","8200","8100","8000"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Call the left side S(x). For x \u2265 2, S(x) = x(x+1)f(x), and for x \u2265 3, S(x\u22121) = (x\u22121)x f(x\u22121).
Step 2: S(x) \u2212 S(x\u22121) = x f(x). So x f(x) = x(x+1)f(x) \u2212 (x\u22121)x f(x\u22121).
Step 3: Divide by x: f(x) = (x+1)f(x) \u2212 (x\u22121)f(x\u22121), so x f(x) = (x\u22121) f(x\u22121) for x \u2265 3.
Step 4: So x f(x) is the same number for all x \u2265 2. Find it at x = 2: 1 + 2f(2) = 2\xB73\xB7f(2) gives f(2) = 1/4, so 2f(2) = 1/2.
Step 5: Hence f(x) = 1/(2x) for x \u2265 2, and 1/f(x) = 2x.
Step 6: 1/f(2022) + 1/f(2028) = 4044 + 4056 = 8100.
Answer: 8100, option (c).`},{pn:87,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The range of the function f(x) = \u221A(3 \u2212 x) + \u221A(2 + x) is",o:["[2\u221A2, \u221A11]","[\u221A5, \u221A10]","[\u221A5, \u221A13]","[\u221A2, \u221A7]"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Both roots must be real: 3 \u2212 x \u2265 0 and 2 + x \u2265 0, so the domain is [\u22122, 3].
Step 2: Square f: f\xB2 = (3 \u2212 x) + (2 + x) + 2\u221A((3 \u2212 x)(2 + x)) = 5 + 2\u221A(\u2212x\xB2 + x + 6).
Step 3: On [\u22122, 3], \u2212x\xB2 + x + 6 is 0 at the ends and has maximum 25/4 at x = 1/2. So its root ranges over [0, 5/2].
Step 4: So f\xB2 ranges over [5 + 0, 5 + 2\xB7(5/2)] = [5, 10].
Step 5: f is positive, so f ranges over [\u221A5, \u221A10].
Answer: option (b).`},{pn:88,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"If the domain of the function f(x) = [x]/(1 + x\xB2), where [x] is the greatest integer \u2264 x, is [2, 6), then its range is",o:["(5/37, 2/5]","(5/26, 2/5] \u2212 \uFF5B9/29, 27/109, 18/89, 9/53\uFF5D","(5/26, 2/5]","(5/37, 2/5] \u2212 \uFF5B9/29, 27/109, 18/89, 9/53\uFF5D"],c:0,s:`Step 1: On [2, 6), [x] takes values 2, 3, 4, 5, so we look at four pieces.
Step 2: Each piece is k/(1 + x\xB2) with k fixed, which decreases as x grows (x > 0).
Step 3: x \u2208 [2, 3): values from 2/5 (at x = 2, included) down to 2/10 (not reached): (1/5, 2/5].
Step 4: x \u2208 [3, 4): 3/10 down to 3/17: (3/17, 3/10]. x \u2208 [4, 5): 4/17 down to 4/26: (2/13, 4/17]. x \u2208 [5, 6): 5/26 down to 5/37: (5/37, 5/26].
Step 5: In decimals: (0.135, 0.192], (0.154, 0.235], (0.176, 0.3], (0.2, 0.4]. Each overlaps the next, so there are no gaps and the union is one interval.
Step 6: Smallest value is just above 5/37 (not reached) and largest is 2/5 (reached).
Answer: (5/37, 2/5], option (a).`},{pn:89,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2212 \uFF5B2, 6\uFF5D \u2192 \u211D be a real valued function defined as f(x) = (x\xB2 + 2x + 1)/(x\xB2 \u2212 8x + 12). Then the range of f is",o:["(\u2212\u221E, \u221221/4] \u222A [21/4, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u221221/4] \u222A [0, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u221221/4) \u222A (0, \u221E)","(\u2212\u221E, \u221221/4] \u222A [1, \u221E)"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Put y = f(x) and clear the denominator: y(x\xB2 \u2212 8x + 12) = (x + 1)\xB2, i.e. (y \u2212 1)x\xB2 \u2212 (8y + 2)x + (12y \u2212 1) = 0.
Step 2: If y = 1 the equation is \u221210x + 11 = 0, giving x = 11/10, which is allowed. So y = 1 is in the range.
Step 3: If y \u2260 1, x is real only if the discriminant is \u2265 0: (8y + 2)\xB2 \u2212 4(y \u2212 1)(12y \u2212 1) \u2265 0.
Step 4: Expand: 64y\xB2 + 32y + 4 \u2212 48y\xB2 + 52y \u2212 4 = 16y\xB2 + 84y \u2265 0, i.e. y(4y + 21) \u2265 0, so y \u2264 \u221221/4 or y \u2265 0.
Step 5: Check x = 2 and x = 6 never give a value: putting x = 2 into the quadratic gives \u22129 \u2260 0 (similarly for 6), so no y is lost.
Step 6: Range: (\u2212\u221E, \u221221/4] \u222A [0, \u221E) (this includes y = 1).
Answer: option (b). (The PDF gives no key for this question.)`},{pn:90,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Counting functions",q:"The total number of functions, f: \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D \u2192 \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\uFF5D such that f(1) + f(2) = f(3), is equal to",o:["60","90","108","126"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Choose f(1) = a and f(2) = b first. Then f(3) = a + b is forced, and it must be at most 6.
Step 2: Count pairs (a, b) with a, b \u2265 1 and a + b \u2264 6: sum 2 has 1 pair, sum 3 has 2, sum 4 has 3, sum 5 has 4, sum 6 has 5.
Step 3: Total pairs = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15.
Step 4: f(4) has no condition, so it can be any of 6 values.
Step 5: Total = 15 \xD7 6 = 90 (a brute-force count also gave 90).
Answer: 90, option (b).`},{pn:91,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) = ax\xB2 + bx + c be such that f(1) = 3, f(\u22122) = \u03BB and f(3) = 4. If f(0) + f(1) + f(\u22122) + f(3) = 14, then \u03BB is equal to",o:["\u22124","13/2","23/2","4"],c:3,s:`Step 1: f(0) = c, f(1) = 3, f(\u22122) = \u03BB, f(3) = 4. The sum condition gives c + 3 + \u03BB + 4 = 14, so c + \u03BB = 7.
Step 2: From f(1): a + b + c = 3. From f(3): 9a + 3b + c = 4. Subtract: 8a + 2b = 1, so b = (1 \u2212 8a)/2.
Step 3: \u03BB = f(\u22122) = 4a \u2212 2b + c. Using c = 3 \u2212 a \u2212 b: \u03BB = 3a \u2212 3b + 3, and c + \u03BB = 6 + 2a \u2212 4b.
Step 4: Set 6 + 2a \u2212 4b = 7, so 2a \u2212 4b = 1. Put b: 2a \u2212 2(1 \u2212 8a) = 1, so 18a = 3 and a = 1/6.
Step 5: b = (1 \u2212 4/3)/2 = \u22121/6 and c = 3 \u2212 1/6 + 1/6 = 3.
Step 6: \u03BB = 4/6 + 2/6 + 3 = 1 + 3 = 4 (check: c + \u03BB = 7).
Answer: 4, option (d).`},{pn:92,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: \u2115 \u2192 \u2115 be a function such that f(m + n) = f(m) + f(n) for every m, n \u2208 \u2115. If f(6) = 18, then f(2)\xB7f(3) is equal to",o:["54","6","36","18"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Let f(1) = k. Then f(2) = f(1) + f(1) = 2k, f(3) = 3k, and in general f(n) = nk.
Step 2: f(6) = 6k = 18, so k = 3.
Step 3: f(2) = 6 and f(3) = 9.
Step 4: f(2)\xB7f(3) = 6 \xD7 9 = 54.
Answer: 54, option (a).`},{pn:93,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"Let [x] denote the greatest integer \u2264 x, where x \u2208 \u211D. If the domain of the real valued function f(x) = \u221A((|[x]| \u2212 2)/(|[x]| \u2212 3)) is (\u2212\u221E, a) \u222A [b, c) \u222A (4, \u221E), a < b < c, then the value of a + b + c is",o:["8","\u22122","\u22123","1"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Let k = |[x]|, a non-negative integer. We need (k \u2212 2)/(k \u2212 3) \u2265 0 with k \u2260 3.
Step 2: Sign check: the fraction is \u2265 0 when k \u2264 2 or k > 3, i.e. k \u2264 2 or k \u2265 4.
Step 3: k \u2264 2 means \u22122 \u2264 [x] \u2264 2, i.e. x \u2208 [\u22122, 3).
Step 4: k \u2265 4 means [x] \u2265 4 (x \u2265 4) or [x] \u2264 \u22124 (x < \u22123).
Step 5: Domain = (\u2212\u221E, \u22123) \u222A [\u22122, 3) \u222A [4, \u221E). Matching with the given form (the printed last piece (4, \u221E) is a small slip for [4, \u221E); it does not change a, b, c): a = \u22123, b = \u22122, c = 3.
Step 6: a + b + c = \u22123 \u2212 2 + 3 = \u22122.
Answer: \u22122, option (b).`},{pn:94,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"If [x] be the greatest integer less than or equal to x, then the sum over n = 8 to 100 of [(\u22121)\u207F n/2] is equal to",o:["2","0","4","\u22122"],c:2,s:`Step 1: For even n, (\u22121)\u207F n/2 = n/2, a whole number, so the bracket gives n/2.
Step 2: For odd n, the value is \u2212n/2, e.g. \u22124.5, and the greatest integer is \u2212(n + 1)/2.
Step 3: Pair an even number 2m with the next odd number 2m + 1: m + (\u2212(2m + 2)/2) = m \u2212 (m + 1) = \u22121.
Step 4: The terms n = 8 to 99 form the pairs (8,9), (10,11), ..., (98,99): that is 46 pairs, giving \u221246.
Step 5: The last term n = 100 is left alone: [50] = 50.
Step 6: Total = \u221246 + 50 = 4 (a direct sum by computer also gave 4).
Answer: 4, option (c).`},{pn:95,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the functions are defined as f(x) = \u221Ax and g(x) = \u221A(1 \u2212 x), then what is the common domain of the following functions: f + g, f \u2212 g, f/g, g/f, g \u2212 f, where (f \xB1 g)(x) = f(x) \xB1 g(x), (f/g)(x) = f(x)/g(x)?",o:["0 \u2264 x < 1","0 < x \u2264 1","0 \u2264 x \u2264 1","0 < x < 1"],c:3,s:`Step 1: f(x) = \u221Ax needs x \u2265 0, and g(x) = \u221A(1 \u2212 x) needs x \u2264 1. So f \xB1 g is defined for 0 \u2264 x \u2264 1.
Step 2: f/g divides by g(x), which must not be 0. g(x) = 0 at x = 1, so we must drop x = 1.
Step 3: g/f divides by f(x), which is 0 at x = 0, so we must drop x = 0.
Step 4: The common domain of all the functions has to satisfy every condition: 0 < x < 1.
Answer: option (d).`},{pn:96,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"A function f(x) is given by f(x) = 5^x/(5^x + 5), then the sum of the series f(1/20) + f(2/20) + f(3/20) + ... + f(39/20) is equal to",o:["19/2","49/2","29/2","39/2"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Find f(2 \u2212 x) = 5^(2\u2212x)/(5^(2\u2212x) + 5) = 25/(25 + 5\xB75^x) = 5/(5 + 5^x).
Step 2: Add: f(x) + f(2 \u2212 x) = (5^x + 5)/(5^x + 5) = 1.
Step 3: In the series the arguments are k/20 and (40 \u2212 k)/20, which add to 2, so those two terms add to 1.
Step 4: Terms k = 1 to 19 pair with k = 39 down to 21: that is 19 pairs, giving 19.
Step 5: The middle term k = 20 is f(1) = 5/(5 + 5) = 1/2.
Step 6: Total = 19 + 1/2 = 39/2.
Answer: 39/2, option (d).`},{pn:97,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be a function which satisfies f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) \u2200 x, y \u2208 \u211D. If f(1) = 2 and g(n) = the sum of f(k) for k = 1 to (n \u2212 1), n \u2208 \u2115, then the value of n, for which g(n) = 20, is",o:["4","9","5","20"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Additivity gives f(2) = f(1) + f(1) = 4, f(3) = 6, and in general f(k) = 2k for natural k.
Step 2: g(n) = 2(1 + 2 + ... + (n \u2212 1)) = 2 \xB7 (n \u2212 1)n/2 = n(n \u2212 1).
Step 3: Set n(n \u2212 1) = 20, i.e. n\xB2 \u2212 n \u2212 20 = 0, so (n \u2212 5)(n + 4) = 0.
Step 4: n is a natural number, so n = 5.
Answer: 5, option (c).`},{pn:98,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x + y) = f(x)\xB7f(y) and the sum over x = 1 to \u221E of f(x) = 2, x, y \u2208 \u2115, where \u2115 is the set of all natural numbers, then the value of f(4)/f(2) is",o:["2/3","1/9","1/3","4/9"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Let f(1) = a. Then f(2) = a\xB2, f(3) = a\xB3, and so f(x) = a\u02E3.
Step 2: The sum a + a\xB2 + a\xB3 + ... is an infinite geometric series with sum a/(1 \u2212 a), valid for |a| < 1.
Step 3: Set a/(1 \u2212 a) = 2, so a = 2 \u2212 2a and a = 2/3.
Step 4: f(4)/f(2) = a\u2074/a\xB2 = a\xB2 = 4/9.
Answer: 4/9, option (d).`},{pn:99,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x) = log_e((1 \u2212 x)/(1 + x)), |x| < 1, then f(2x/(1 + x\xB2)) is equal to:",o:["2f(x\xB2)","\u22122f(x)","(f(x))\xB2","2f(x)"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Replace x by 2x/(1 + x\xB2) in f: the fraction becomes (1 \u2212 2x/(1+x\xB2)) / (1 + 2x/(1+x\xB2)).
Step 2: Multiply top and bottom by (1 + x\xB2): ((1 + x\xB2) \u2212 2x)/((1 + x\xB2) + 2x).
Step 3: Recognise the perfect squares: (1 \u2212 x)\xB2/(1 + x)\xB2.
Step 4: So f(2x/(1+x\xB2)) = log_e[((1 \u2212 x)/(1 + x))\xB2] = 2 log_e((1 \u2212 x)/(1 + x)) (using log of a square).
Step 5: That equals 2f(x).
Answer: option (d).`},{pn:100,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"Let f(x) = a\u02E3 (a > 0) be written as f(x) = f\u2081(x) + f\u2082(x), where f\u2081(x) is an even function and f\u2082(x) is an odd function. Then f\u2081(x + y) + f\u2081(x \u2212 y) equals",o:["2f\u2081(x)f\u2081(y)","2f\u2081(x + y)f\u2082(x \u2212 y)","2f\u2081(x)f\u2082(y)","2f\u2081(x + y)f\u2081(x \u2212 y)"],c:0,s:`Step 1: An even part is f\u2081(x) = (f(x) + f(\u2212x))/2, so f\u2081(x) = (a\u02E3 + a\u207B\u02E3)/2, and the odd part is f\u2082(x) = (a\u02E3 \u2212 a\u207B\u02E3)/2.
Step 2: f\u2081(x + y) + f\u2081(x \u2212 y) = [a^(x+y) + a^(\u2212x\u2212y) + a^(x\u2212y) + a^(\u2212x+y)]/2.
Step 3: Group the four terms: (a\u02E3 + a\u207B\u02E3)(a\u02B8 + a\u207B\u02B8) = a^(x+y) + a^(x\u2212y) + a^(\u2212x+y) + a^(\u2212x\u2212y). That is exactly the numerator.
Step 4: So the sum = (a\u02E3 + a\u207B\u02E3)(a\u02B8 + a\u207B\u02B8)/2 = (2f\u2081(x))(2f\u2081(y))/2.
Step 5: = 2f\u2081(x)f\u2081(y).
Answer: option (a).`},{pn:101,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let \u03A3(k=1 to 10) f(a+k) = 16(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1), where the function f satisfies f(x+y) = f(x)f(y) for all natural numbers x, y and f(1) = 2. Then the natural number 'a' is",o:["4","2","16","3"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Put y = 1 in f(x+y) = f(x)f(y): f(x+1) = 2\xB7f(x). So each value is double the previous one.
Step 2: Since f(1) = 2, we get f(2) = 4, f(3) = 8, so f(n) = 2\u207F for every natural n.
Step 3: Then f(a+k) = 2^(a+k) = 2\u1D43\xB72\u1D4F.
Step 4: Sum over k = 1 to 10: 2\u1D43(2 + 2\xB2 + ... + 2\xB9\u2070). This is a geometric series with sum 2(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1).
Step 5: So the left side = 2\u1D43\xB72\xB7(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1) = 2^(a+1)(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1).
Step 6: Equate with 16(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1) = 2\u2074(2\xB9\u2070 \u2212 1): a + 1 = 4, so a = 3.
Answer: (d) 3`},{pn:102,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of the definition of the function f(x) = 1/(4 \u2212 x\xB2) + log\u2081\u2080(x\xB3 \u2212 x) is",o:["(\u22122, \u22121) \u222A (\u22121, 0) \u222A (2, \u221E)","(1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E)","(\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E)","(\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2) \u222A (3, \u221E)"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Two conditions must hold: the fraction needs a non-zero denominator, and the logarithm needs a positive argument.
Step 2: Denominator: 4 \u2212 x\xB2 \u2260 0, so x \u2260 2 and x \u2260 \u22122.
Step 3: Logarithm: x\xB3 \u2212 x > 0, i.e. x(x \u2212 1)(x + 1) > 0.
Step 4: The roots are \u22121, 0, 1. Test the sign in each gap: for x > 1 it is positive; for 0 < x < 1 negative; for \u22121 < x < 0 positive; for x < \u22121 negative.
Step 5: So x\xB3 \u2212 x > 0 gives (\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, \u221E).
Step 6: Remove x = 2 (x = \u22122 is already outside): (\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E).
Answer: (c)`},{pn:103,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be defined by f(x) = x/(1 + x\xB2), x \u2208 \u211D. Then the range of f is",o:["(\u22121, 1) \u2212 \uFF5B0\uFF5D","\u211D \u2212 [\u22121, 1]","[\u22121/2, 1/2]","\u211D \u2212 [\u22121/2, 1/2]"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Let y = x/(1 + x\xB2). We find which y values are possible.
Step 2: Cross-multiply: y x\xB2 \u2212 x + y = 0, a quadratic in x.
Step 3: If y = 0, x = 0 works, so y = 0 is in the range.
Step 4: If y \u2260 0, x is real only when the discriminant is \u2265 0: 1 \u2212 4y\xB2 \u2265 0.
Step 5: So y\xB2 \u2264 1/4, i.e. \u22121/2 \u2264 y \u2264 1/2 (this already includes 0).
Answer: (c) [\u22121/2, 1/2]`},{pn:104,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x) + 2f(1/x) = 3x, x \u2260 0, and S = \uFF5Bx \u2208 \u211D : f(x) = f(\u2212x)\uFF5D; then S",o:["is an empty set","contains exactly one element","contains exactly two elements","contains more than two elements"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Replace x by 1/x in the given equation: f(1/x) + 2f(x) = 3/x.
Step 2: Now we have two equations: f(x) + 2f(1/x) = 3x and 2f(x) + f(1/x) = 3/x. Treat f(x) and f(1/x) as unknowns.
Step 3: Multiply the second by 2: 4f(x) + 2f(1/x) = 6/x. Subtract the first: 3f(x) = 6/x \u2212 3x.
Step 4: So f(x) = 2/x \u2212 x.
Step 5: f(\u2212x) = \u22122/x + x = \u2212f(x), so f is odd.
Step 6: f(x) = f(\u2212x) means f(x) = \u2212f(x), so f(x) = 0: 2/x \u2212 x = 0, x\xB2 = 2.
Step 7: x = \u221A2 or x = \u2212\u221A2, so S has exactly two elements.
Answer: (c)`},{pn:105,y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of the function f(x) = 1/\u221A(|x| \u2212 x) is",o:["(\u2212\u221E, 0)","(\u2212\u221E, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B0\uFF5D","(\u2212\u221E, \u221E)","(0, \u221E)"],c:0,s:`Step 1: The expression is under a square root and also in a denominator, so |x| \u2212 x must be strictly greater than 0.
Step 2: If x \u2265 0, then |x| = x and |x| \u2212 x = 0. This is not allowed.
Step 3: If x < 0, then |x| = \u2212x and |x| \u2212 x = \u22122x > 0. This is allowed.
Step 4: So the domain is all x < 0.
Answer: (a) (\u2212\u221E, 0)`},{pn:106,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"A real valued function f(x) satisfies the functional equation f(x \u2212 y) = f(x)f(y) \u2212 f(a \u2212 x)f(a + y) where a is a given constant and f(0) = 1. f(2a \u2212 x) is equal to",o:["f(x)","\u2212f(x)","f(\u2212x)","f(a) + f(a \u2212 x)"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Put x = 0, y = 0: f(0) = f(0)\xB2 \u2212 f(a)\xB7f(a). With f(0) = 1 this gives 1 = 1 \u2212 f(a)\xB2, so f(a) = 0.
Step 2: To get 2a \u2212 x, choose x \u2192 a and y \u2192 x \u2212 a. Then x \u2212 y becomes a \u2212 (x \u2212 a) = 2a \u2212 x.
Step 3: Left side: f(2a \u2212 x).
Step 4: Right side: f(a)f(x \u2212 a) \u2212 f(a \u2212 a)f(a + x \u2212 a) = f(a)f(x \u2212 a) \u2212 f(0)f(x).
Step 5: Use f(a) = 0 and f(0) = 1: right side = 0 \u2212 f(x) = \u2212f(x).
Answer: (b) \u2212f(x)`},{pn:107,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The range of the function f(x) = \u2077\u207B\u02E3P(x\u22123) is",o:["\uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4\uFF5D","\uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\uFF5D","\uFF5B1, 2, 3\uFF5D","\uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5\uFF5D"],c:2,s:`Step 1: \u207FPr is defined only when n and r are whole numbers with 0 \u2264 r \u2264 n. Here n = 7 \u2212 x and r = x \u2212 3.
Step 2: x \u2212 3 \u2265 0 gives x \u2265 3. Also x \u2212 3 \u2264 7 \u2212 x gives 2x \u2264 10, so x \u2264 5.
Step 3: So the domain is x = 3, 4, 5.
Step 4: x = 3: \u2074P\u2080 = 1.
Step 5: x = 4: \xB3P\u2081 = 3.
Step 6: x = 5: \xB2P\u2082 = 2.
Step 7: The range is \uFF5B1, 3, 2\uFF5D = \uFF5B1, 2, 3\uFF5D.
Answer: (c)`},{pn:108,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"The function f(x) = log(x + \u221A(x\xB2 + 1)) is",o:["an odd function","a periodic function","neither an even nor an odd function","an even function"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Check f(\u2212x) = log(\u2212x + \u221A(x\xB2 + 1)).
Step 2: Multiply and divide by (x + \u221A(x\xB2 + 1)): (\u221A(x\xB2+1) \u2212 x)(\u221A(x\xB2+1) + x) = (x\xB2 + 1) \u2212 x\xB2 = 1.
Step 3: So \u2212x + \u221A(x\xB2+1) = 1/(x + \u221A(x\xB2+1)).
Step 4: f(\u2212x) = log[1/(x + \u221A(x\xB2+1))] = \u2212log(x + \u221A(x\xB2+1)) = \u2212f(x).
Step 5: f(\u2212x) = \u2212f(x) means f is odd. (It is not periodic since it keeps increasing.)
Answer: (a) an odd function`},{pn:109,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Domain of definition of the function f(x) = 3/(4 \u2212 x\xB2) + log\u2081\u2080(x\xB3 \u2212 x), is",o:["(\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2)","(1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E)","(\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E)","(1, 2)"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Denominator must not be zero: 4 \u2212 x\xB2 \u2260 0, so x \u2260 \xB12.
Step 2: The log needs x\xB3 \u2212 x > 0, i.e. x(x \u2212 1)(x + 1) > 0.
Step 3: With roots \u22121, 0, 1, the sign pattern gives positive values on (\u22121, 0) and on (1, \u221E).
Step 4: Remove x = 2 from (1, \u221E). (x = \u22122 is not in (\u22121, 0) anyway.)
Step 5: Domain = (\u22121, 0) \u222A (1, 2) \u222A (2, \u221E).
Answer: (c)`},{pn:110,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D satisfies f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y), for all x, y \u2208 \u211D and f(1) = 7, then \u03A3(r=1 to n) f(r) is",o:["7(n+1)/2","7n(n+1)","7n(n+1)/2","7n/2"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Put y = 1: f(x + 1) = f(x) + 7. Each step of 1 adds 7.
Step 2: Starting from f(1) = 7, we get f(2) = 14, f(3) = 21, so f(r) = 7r for natural r.
Step 3: \u03A3 f(r) = 7(1 + 2 + ... + n).
Step 4: Sum of the first n natural numbers is n(n + 1)/2.
Step 5: So the sum = 7n(n + 1)/2.
Answer: (c)`},{pn:111,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Range of the function f(x) = (x\xB2 + x + 2)/(x\xB2 + x + 1); x \u2208 \u211D is",o:["(1, \u221E)","(1, 11/7]","(1, 7/3]","(1, 7/5)"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Divide the top: (x\xB2 + x + 2) = (x\xB2 + x + 1) + 1, so f(x) = 1 + 1/(x\xB2 + x + 1).
Step 2: Write x\xB2 + x + 1 = (x + 1/2)\xB2 + 3/4. Its smallest value is 3/4 (at x = \u22121/2) and it can be as large as we like.
Step 3: So x\xB2 + x + 1 takes every value in [3/4, \u221E).
Step 4: Then 1/(x\xB2 + x + 1) takes values in (0, 4/3] (never 0, largest 4/3).
Step 5: Add 1: f(x) lies in (1, 1 + 4/3] = (1, 7/3].
Answer: (c)`},{pn:112,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"Which one is not periodic?",o:["|sin 3x| + sin\xB2x","cos \u221Ax + cos\xB2x","cos 4x + tan\xB2x","cos 2x + sin x"],c:1,s:`Step 1: A sum of periodic functions is periodic if each part is periodic (a common period exists).
Step 2: (a) |sin 3x| has period \u03C0/3 and sin\xB2x has period \u03C0, so the sum has period \u03C0. Periodic.
Step 3: (c) cos 4x has period \u03C0/2 and tan\xB2x has period \u03C0, so the sum has period \u03C0. Periodic.
Step 4: (d) cos 2x has period \u03C0 and sin x has period 2\u03C0, so the sum has period 2\u03C0. Periodic.
Step 5: (b) cos \u221Ax is not periodic: the gaps between its zeros keep growing as x grows, so no fixed period exists. Hence the sum is not periodic.
Answer: (b)`},{pn:113,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"The period of sin\xB2\u03B8 is",o:["\u03C0\xB2","\u03C0","\u03C0\xB3","\u03C0/2"],c:1,s:`Step 1: Use sin\xB2\u03B8 = (1 \u2212 cos 2\u03B8)/2.
Step 2: The constant 1/2 does not change the period. Only cos 2\u03B8 matters.
Step 3: cos(k\u03B8) has period 2\u03C0/k, so cos 2\u03B8 has period 2\u03C0/2 = \u03C0.
Step 4: Check: sin\xB2(\u03B8 + \u03C0) = (\u2212sin \u03B8)\xB2 = sin\xB2\u03B8, and no smaller positive shift works.
Answer: (b) \u03C0`},{pn:114,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"func",st:"Composition and inverse",q:"Suppose f(x) = (x + 1)\xB2 for x \u2265 \u22121. If g(x) is the function whose graph is the reflection of the graph of f(x) with respect to the line y = x, then g(x) equals",o:["\u2212\u221Ax \u2212 1, x \u2265 0","1/(x + 1)\xB2, x > \u22121","\u221Ax + 1, x \u2265 \u22121","\u221Ax \u2212 1, x \u2265 0"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Reflecting a graph in the line y = x gives the graph of the inverse function. So g = f\u207B\xB9.
Step 2: Let y = (x + 1)\xB2. For x \u2265 \u22121 we have x + 1 \u2265 0, so x + 1 = \u221Ay (the positive root).
Step 3: So x = \u221Ay \u2212 1. Swap the names: g(x) = \u221Ax \u2212 1.
Step 4: The domain of g is the range of f. Since (x + 1)\xB2 \u2265 0, the range of f is [0, \u221E), so x \u2265 0.
Answer: (d) \u221Ax \u2212 1, x \u2265 0`},{pn:115,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of definition of f(x) = log\u2082(x + 3)/(x\xB2 + 3x + 2) is",o:["\u211D \u2212 \uFF5B\u22121, \u22122\uFF5D","(\u22122, \u221E)","\u211D \u2212 \uFF5B\u22121, \u22122, \u22123\uFF5D","(\u22123, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B\u22121, \u22122\uFF5D"],c:3,s:`Step 1: The log needs a positive argument: x + 3 > 0, so x > \u22123.
Step 2: The denominator must not be zero: x\xB2 + 3x + 2 = (x + 1)(x + 2) \u2260 0, so x \u2260 \u22121 and x \u2260 \u22122.
Step 3: Both \u22121 and \u22122 lie in (\u22123, \u221E), so they must be removed.
Step 4: Domain = (\u22123, \u221E) \u2212 \uFF5B\u22121, \u22122\uFF5D.
Answer: (d)`},{pn:116,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of definition of the function f(x) given by the equation 2\u02E3 + 2\u02B8 = 2 is",o:["0 < x \u2264 1","0 \u2264 x \u2264 1","\u2212\u221E < x \u2264 0","\u2212\u221E < x < 1"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Here y is a function of x. Solve for y: 2\u02B8 = 2 \u2212 2\u02E3.
Step 2: A power of 2 is always positive, so 2\u02B8 > 0, which needs 2 \u2212 2\u02E3 > 0.
Step 3: So 2\u02E3 < 2 = 2\xB9, and since 2\u02E3 is increasing, x < 1.
Step 4: For every x < 1, 2 \u2212 2\u02E3 > 0 and y = log\u2082(2 \u2212 2\u02E3) exists. There is no lower limit on x.
Answer: (d) \u2212\u221E < x < 1`},{pn:117,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let f(\u03B8) = sin \u03B8 (sin \u03B8 + sin 3\u03B8). Then f(\u03B8)",o:["\u2265 0 only when \u03B8 \u2265 0","\u2264 0 for all real \u03B8","\u2265 0 for all real \u03B8","\u2264 0 only when \u03B8 \u2264 0"],c:2,s:`Step 1: Use sin A + sin B = 2 sin((A+B)/2) cos((A\u2212B)/2). So sin \u03B8 + sin 3\u03B8 = 2 sin 2\u03B8 cos \u03B8.
Step 2: Then f(\u03B8) = sin \u03B8 \xB7 2 sin 2\u03B8 cos \u03B8 = (2 sin \u03B8 cos \u03B8) \xB7 sin 2\u03B8.
Step 3: Since 2 sin \u03B8 cos \u03B8 = sin 2\u03B8, f(\u03B8) = sin 2\u03B8 \xB7 sin 2\u03B8 = sin\xB22\u03B8.
Step 4: A square is never negative, so f(\u03B8) \u2265 0 for every real \u03B8.
Answer: (c)`},{pn:118,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"Which of the following functions is periodic?",o:["f(x) = x \u2212 [x], where [x] denotes the greatest integer less than or equal to the real number x","f(x) = sin(1/x), x \u2260 0, f(0) = 0","f(x) = x cos x","none of these"],c:0,s:`Step 1: Test (a): [x + 1] = [x] + 1, so f(x + 1) = x + 1 \u2212 [x] \u2212 1 = x \u2212 [x] = f(x). So f has period 1 (it is the fractional part).
Step 2: (b) sin(1/x): its zeros at x = 1/(n\u03C0) get closer and closer to 0, so the pattern never repeats with a fixed length. Not periodic.
Step 3: (c) x cos x: the amplitude grows with x, so values cannot repeat after a fixed shift. Not periodic.
Step 4: Since (a) is periodic, (d) is wrong.
Answer: (a)`},{pn:119,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"The domain of definition of the function y = 1/log\u2081\u2080(1 \u2212 x) + \u221A(x + 2) is",o:["(\u22123, \u22122) excluding \u22122.5","[0, 1] excluding 0.5","[\u22122, 1) excluding 0","none of these"],c:2,s:`Step 1: The logarithm needs 1 \u2212 x > 0, so x < 1.
Step 2: It is in a denominator, so log\u2081\u2080(1 \u2212 x) \u2260 0, i.e. 1 \u2212 x \u2260 1, so x \u2260 0.
Step 3: The square root needs x + 2 \u2265 0, so x \u2265 \u22122.
Step 4: Combine: \u22122 \u2264 x < 1, with x = 0 removed.
Answer: (c) [\u22122, 1) excluding 0`},{pn:120,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If f(x) = cos(log x), then f(x)f(y) \u2212 \xBD[f(x/y) + f(xy)] has the value",o:["\u22121","1/2","\u22122","none of these"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Let A = log x and B = log y.
Step 2: log(x/y) = A \u2212 B and log(xy) = A + B. So f(x/y) = cos(A \u2212 B) and f(xy) = cos(A + B).
Step 3: cos(A \u2212 B) + cos(A + B) = 2 cos A cos B.
Step 4: So \xBD[f(x/y) + f(xy)] = cos A cos B.
Step 5: f(x)f(y) = cos A cos B. The expression = cos A cos B \u2212 cos A cos B = 0.
Step 6: 0 is not \u22121, 1/2 or \u22122, so the answer is none of these.
Answer: (d) (the value is 0)`},{pn:121,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) = |x \u2212 1|. Then",o:["f(x\xB2) = (f(x))\xB2","f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y)","f(|x|) = |f(x)|","none of these"],c:3,s:`Step 1: Test each statement with a simple number. Here f(x) = |x \u2212 1|.
Step 2: (a) Take x = 2: f(4) = 3 but (f(2))\xB2 = 1\xB2 = 1. Not equal, so (a) is false.
Step 3: (b) Take x = 2, y = 2: f(4) = 3 but f(2) + f(2) = 2. Not equal, so (b) is false.
Step 4: (c) Take x = \u22122: f(|\u22122|) = f(2) = 1, but |f(\u22122)| = |\u22123| = 3. Not equal, so (c) is false.
Step 5: All of (a), (b), (c) fail for some value.
Answer: (d) none of these`},{pn:122,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: (\u22121, 1) \u2192 \u211D be such that f(cos 4\u03B8) = 2/(2 \u2212 sec\xB2\u03B8) for \u03B8 \u2208 (0, \u03C0/4) \u222A (\u03C0/4, \u03C0/2). Then the values of f(1/3) is (are)",o:["1 \u2212 \u221A(3/2)","1 + \u221A(3/2)","1 \u2212 \u221A(2/3)","1 + \u221A(2/3)"],c:[0,1],s:`Step 1: Write everything in terms of c = cos 2\u03B8. We have sec\xB2\u03B8 = 1/cos\xB2\u03B8 and cos\xB2\u03B8 = (1 + cos 2\u03B8)/2 = (1 + c)/2, so sec\xB2\u03B8 = 2/(1 + c).
Step 2: 2 \u2212 sec\xB2\u03B8 = 2 \u2212 2/(1 + c) = 2c/(1 + c). So f(cos 4\u03B8) = 2(1 + c)/(2c) = (1 + c)/c = 1 + 1/c.
Step 3: Now cos 4\u03B8 = 2cos\xB22\u03B8 \u2212 1 = 2c\xB2 \u2212 1. We need cos 4\u03B8 = 1/3.
Step 4: 2c\xB2 \u2212 1 = 1/3 gives c\xB2 = 2/3, so c = \xB1\u221A(2/3).
Step 5: Both signs are possible: for \u03B8 in (0, \u03C0/4), 2\u03B8 is in (0, \u03C0/2) so c > 0; for \u03B8 in (\u03C0/4, \u03C0/2), 2\u03B8 is in (\u03C0/2, \u03C0) so c < 0.
Step 6: f(1/3) = 1 + 1/c = 1 \xB1 \u221A(3/2).
Answer: (a) and (b)`},{pn:123,y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"If f(x) = cos[\u03C0\xB2]x + cos[\u2212\u03C0\xB2]x, where [x] stands for the greatest integer function, then",o:["f(\u03C0/2) = \u22121","f(\u03C0) = 1","f(\u2212\u03C0) = 0","f(\u03C0/4) = 1"],c:[0,2],s:`Step 1: \u03C0\xB2 \u2248 9.87, so [\u03C0\xB2] = 9. Also \u2212\u03C0\xB2 \u2248 \u22129.87, and the greatest integer not above it is \u221210, so [\u2212\u03C0\xB2] = \u221210.
Step 2: So f(x) = cos 9x + cos 10x.
Step 3: (a) f(\u03C0/2) = cos(9\u03C0/2) + cos 5\u03C0 = 0 + (\u22121) = \u22121. True.
Step 4: (b) f(\u03C0) = cos 9\u03C0 + cos 10\u03C0 = \u22121 + 1 = 0, not 1. False.
Step 5: (c) f(\u2212\u03C0) = cos(\u22129\u03C0) + cos(\u221210\u03C0) = \u22121 + 1 = 0. True.
Step 6: (d) f(\u03C0/4) = cos(9\u03C0/4) + cos(10\u03C0/4) = \u221A2/2 + 0 = \u221A2/2, not 1. False.
Answer: (a) and (c)`},{pn:124,y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let g(x) be a function defined on [\u22121, 1]. If the area of the equilateral triangle with two of its vertices at (0, 0) and [x, g(x)] is \u221A3/4, then the function g(x) is",o:["g(x) = \xB1\u221A(1 \u2212 x\xB2)","g(x) = \u221A(1 \u2212 x\xB2)","g(x) = \u2212\u221A(1 \u2212 x\xB2)","g(x) = \u221A(1 + x\xB2)"],c:[1,2],s:`Step 1: The side of the triangle is the distance from (0, 0) to (x, g(x)), so side\xB2 = x\xB2 + [g(x)]\xB2.
Step 2: Area of an equilateral triangle = (\u221A3/4)\xB7side\xB2.
Step 3: Given area = \u221A3/4, so side\xB2 = 1, i.e. x\xB2 + [g(x)]\xB2 = 1.
Step 4: So [g(x)]\xB2 = 1 \u2212 x\xB2 and g(x) = \xB1\u221A(1 \u2212 x\xB2), which is defined on [\u22121, 1].
Step 5: A function must give one value for each x, so g can be the + root or the \u2212 root: (b) and (c) are both valid functions. (a) with \xB1 is two values at once, and (d) gives x\xB2 + g\xB2 = 1 + 2x\xB2 \u2260 1.
Answer: (b) and (c) (matches the printed key b, c)`},{pn:125,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If a function f satisfies f(m + n) = f(m) + f(n) for all m, n \u2208 \u2115 and f(1) = 1, then the largest natural number \u03BB such that \u03A3(k=1 to 2022) f(\u03BB + k) \u2264 (2022)\xB2 is equal to ______.",n:1010,s:`Step 1: Put n = 1: f(m + 1) = f(m) + 1. With f(1) = 1 this gives f(n) = n for every natural n.
Step 2: So f(\u03BB + k) = \u03BB + k.
Step 3: \u03A3(k=1 to 2022)(\u03BB + k) = 2022\u03BB + 2022\xB72023/2.
Step 4: Require 2022\u03BB + 2022\xB72023/2 \u2264 2022\xB2. Divide by 2022: \u03BB + 2023/2 \u2264 2022.
Step 5: \u03BB \u2264 2022 \u2212 1011.5 = 1010.5.
Step 6: The largest natural number is 1010.
Answer: 1010`},{pn:126,y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be a function such that f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) for all x, y \u2208 \u211D, and g: \u211D \u2192 (0, \u221E) be a function such that g(x + y) = g(x)g(y) for all x, y \u2208 \u211D. If f(-3/5) = 12 and g(-1/3) = 2, then the value of (f(1/4) + g(-2) - 8)g(0) is ______.",n:51,s:`Step 1: f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) means f(kx) = k\xB7f(x) for rational k. So f(-3/5) = -3\xB7f(1/5) = 12 gives f(1/5) = -4.
Step 2: Then f(1) = 5\xB7f(1/5) = -20, and f(1/4) = (1/4)\xB7f(1) = -5.
Step 3: g(x + y) = g(x)g(y) means g(kx) = g(x)^k for positive integer k. Since -2 = 6 \xD7 (-1/3), g(-2) = g(-1/3)^6 = 2^6 = 64.
Step 4: g(0) = g(0 + 0) = g(0)\xB2, and g(0) > 0, so g(0) = 1.
Step 5: Put the values in: (-5 + 64 - 8) \xD7 1 = 51.
Answer: 51`},{pn:127,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"If the domain of the function log_e((6x\xB2 + 5x + 1)/(2x - 1)) + cos\u207B\xB9((2x\xB2 - 3x + 4)/(3x - 5)) is (\u03B1, \u03B2) \u222A (\u03B3, \u03B4], then 18(\u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 + \u03B3\xB2 + \u03B4\xB2) is equal to ______.",n:20,s:`Step 1: For the log we need (6x\xB2 + 5x + 1)/(2x - 1) > 0. Factor: (2x + 1)(3x + 1)/(2x - 1) > 0.
Step 2: Critical points are -1/2, -1/3, 1/2. Checking signs on each interval, the fraction is positive for x \u2208 (-1/2, -1/3) \u222A (1/2, \u221E).
Step 3: For cos\u207B\xB9 we need -1 \u2264 (2x\xB2 - 3x + 4)/(3x - 5) \u2264 1. The numerator 2x\xB2 - 3x + 4 is always positive (its discriminant 9 - 32 < 0).
Step 4: If 3x - 5 > 0, we need 2x\xB2 - 3x + 4 \u2264 3x - 5, i.e. 2x\xB2 - 6x + 9 \u2264 0, which never holds (discriminant 36 - 72 < 0). So 3x - 5 < 0 (x < 5/3).
Step 5: For 3x - 5 < 0 the fraction is negative, so we need 2x\xB2 - 3x + 4 \u2264 5 - 3x, i.e. 2x\xB2 \u2264 1, so -1/\u221A2 \u2264 x \u2264 1/\u221A2.
Step 6: Intersect with Step 2: (-1/2, -1/3) \u222A (1/2, 1/\u221A2]. So \u03B1 = -1/2, \u03B2 = -1/3, \u03B3 = 1/2, \u03B4 = 1/\u221A2.
Step 7: \u03B1\xB2 + \u03B2\xB2 + \u03B3\xB2 + \u03B4\xB2 = 1/4 + 1/9 + 1/4 + 1/2 = 10/9, and 18 \xD7 10/9 = 20.
Answer: 20`},{pn:128,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let a, b, c be three distinct positive real numbers such that (2a)^(log_e a) = (bc)^(log_e b) and b^(log_e 2) = a^(log_e c). Then 6a + 5bc is equal to ______.",n:8,s:`Note: the question as printed is not fully determined; the intended case (a = 1/2, bc = 1) gives the printed key 8. The other case a = 1/b, c = 1/2 also satisfies both equations but gives a non-constant value.
Step 1: Take natural logs and write A = ln a, B = ln b, C = ln c, L = ln 2. The equations become (L + A)A = (B + C)B and L\xB7B = A\xB7C.
Step 2: From the second, C = LB/A. Substitute in the first: LA + A\xB2 = B\xB2 + LB\xB2/A. Multiply by A: A\xB2(A + L) = B\xB2(A + L).
Step 3: So (A + L)(A\xB2 - B\xB2) = 0. Either A = -L, or A = \xB1B. A = B would make a = b, which is not allowed (distinct).
Step 4: Taking A = -L gives a = 1/2. Then C = LB/A = -B, so c = 1/b and bc = 1.
Step 5: 6a + 5bc = 6 \xD7 1/2 + 5 \xD7 1 = 3 + 5 = 8.
Answer: 8`},{pn:129,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Counting functions",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5\uFF5D and B = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\uFF5D. Then the number of functions f: A \u2192 B satisfying f(1) + f(2) = f(4) - 1 is equal to ______.",n:360,s:`Step 1: A function picks one value in B for each of 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. The condition links only f(1), f(2), f(4).
Step 2: f(4) = f(1) + f(2) + 1 must be at most 6, so f(1) + f(2) \u2264 5. Then f(4) is fixed by f(1) and f(2).
Step 3: Count pairs (f(1), f(2)) with values from 1 to 6 and sum \u2264 5: sum 2 has 1 pair, sum 3 has 2, sum 4 has 3, sum 5 has 4. Total 10.
Step 4: f(3) and f(5) are free: 6 choices each, 6 \xD7 6 = 36.
Step 5: Total = 10 \xD7 36 = 360.
Answer: 360`},{pn:130,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"Let [a] denote the greatest integer \u2264 a. Then [\u221A1] + [\u221A2] + [\u221A3] + ... + [\u221A120] is equal to ______.",n:825,s:`Step 1: [\u221Ak] = n exactly when n\xB2 \u2264 k < (n+1)\xB2, that is k runs from n\xB2 to n\xB2 + 2n. So the value n appears 2n + 1 times.
Step 2: For n = 1 to 9 all the numbers k up to 99 are covered. For n = 10, k runs from 100 to 120, which is 21 numbers (also 2n + 1 = 21, fully covered).
Step 3: Sum = \u03A3 n(2n + 1) for n = 1 to 10 = 2\u03A3n\xB2 + \u03A3n.
Step 4: \u03A3n\xB2 = 385 and \u03A3n = 55, so the sum = 2 \xD7 385 + 55 = 825.
Answer: 825`},{pn:131,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Suppose f is a function satisfying f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) for all x, y \u2208 \u2115 and f(1) = 1/5. If \u03A3 (n = 1 to m) f(n)/(n(n+1)(n+2)) = 1/12, then m is equal to ______.",n:10,s:`Step 1: f(n) = f(1 + 1 + ... + 1) = n\xB7f(1) = n/5.
Step 2: Each term becomes (n/5)/(n(n+1)(n+2)) = (1/5) \xD7 1/((n+1)(n+2)).
Step 3: Use 1/((n+1)(n+2)) = 1/(n+1) - 1/(n+2); the sum telescopes: \u03A3 = 1/2 - 1/(m+2).
Step 4: So (1/5)(1/2 - 1/(m+2)) = 1/12, giving 1/2 - 1/(m+2) = 5/12.
Step 5: 1/(m+2) = 1/2 - 5/12 = 1/12, so m + 2 = 12.
Answer: m = 10`},{pn:132,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Counting functions",q:"Let A = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 9\uFF5D. Then the number of possible functions f: A \u2192 A such that f(m\xB7n) = f(m)\xB7f(n) for every m, n \u2208 A with m\xB7n \u2208 A is equal to ______.",n:432,s:`Step 1: Put m = n = 1: f(1) = f(1)\xB2. So f(1) is 0 or 1, but 0 is not in A, so f(1) = 1.
Step 2: Find which products m\xB7n lie in A. Besides 1\xB7x = x (which only gives f(x) = f(1)f(x), already true), the only product is 3\xB73 = 9. (2\xB72 = 4, 2\xB73 = 6 etc. are not in A.)
Step 3: So the only real condition is f(9) = f(3)\xB2. f(3)\xB2 must be in A: f(3) = 1 gives 1, f(3) = 3 gives 9; 2, 5, 8, 9 give 4, 25, 64, 81 which are not in A. So f(3) \u2208 \uFF5B1, 3\uFF5D: 2 choices, and f(9) is then fixed.
Step 4: f(2), f(5), f(8) are free: 6 choices each = 216.
Step 5: Total = 2 \xD7 216 = 432 (also confirmed by a brute-force count).
Answer: 432`},{pn:133,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be a function defined by f(x) = 2e^(2x)/(e^(2x) + e). Then f(1/100) + f(2/100) + f(3/100) + ... + f(99/100) is equal to ______.",n:99,s:`Step 1: Look for a pairing: compute f(x) + f(1 - x). Let u = e^(2x), so f(x) = 2u/(u + e).
Step 2: e^(2(1-x)) = e\xB2/u, so f(1 - x) = 2(e\xB2/u)/(e\xB2/u + e) = 2e\xB2/(e\xB2 + eu) = 2e/(e + u).
Step 3: f(x) + f(1 - x) = (2u + 2e)/(u + e) = 2.
Step 4: The 99 terms pair up as k/100 with (100 - k)/100: 49 pairs, each summing to 2, giving 98. The middle term is f(1/2) = 2e/(e + e) = 1.
Step 5: Total = 98 + 1 = 99.
Answer: 99`},{pn:134,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let c, k \u2208 \u211D. If f(x) = (c + 1)x\xB2 + (1 - c\xB2)x + 2k and f(x + y) = f(x) + f(y) - xy, for all x, y \u2208 \u211D, then the value of 2(f(1) + f(2) + f(3) + ... + f(20)) is equal to ______.",n:-3395,s:`Note: the printed key says 3395; working gives -3395 (every f(x) is negative, so the sum cannot be positive). The magnitude agrees.
Step 1: Constant terms: f(x + y) has 2k, f(x) + f(y) has 4k. So 2k = 4k, giving k = 0.
Step 2: x\xB2 terms: with a = c + 1, f(x + y) - f(x) - f(y) = 2a\xB7xy. This must equal -xy, so 2a = -1 and a = -1/2.
Step 3: c + 1 = -1/2 gives c = -3/2. Then 1 - c\xB2 = 1 - 9/4 = -5/4.
Step 4: f(x) = -x\xB2/2 - 5x/4.
Step 5: \u03A3x\xB2 (1 to 20) = 20\xB721\xB741/6 = 2870 and \u03A3x = 210. So \u03A3f = -2870/2 - 5\xB7210/4 = -1435 - 262.5 = -1697.5.
Step 6: 2 \xD7 (-1697.5) = -3395.
Answer: -3395`},{pn:135,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) be a polynomial of degree 3 such that f(k) = -2/k for k = 2, 3, 4, 5. Then the value of 52 - 10f(10) is equal to ______.",n:26,s:`Step 1: Define g(x) = x\xB7f(x) + 2. Since f has degree 3, g has degree 4.
Step 2: For k = 2, 3, 4, 5: g(k) = k\xB7(-2/k) + 2 = 0. So g(x) = a(x - 2)(x - 3)(x - 4)(x - 5).
Step 3: Put x = 0: g(0) = 2, and a(-2)(-3)(-4)(-5) = 120a. So a = 1/60.
Step 4: Put x = 10: 10f(10) + 2 = (1/60)(8)(7)(6)(5) = 1680/60 = 28.
Step 5: So 10f(10) = 26, and 52 - 10f(10) = 26.
Answer: 26`},{pn:136,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"func",st:"Counting functions",q:"Let S = \uFF5B1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7\uFF5D. Then the number of possible functions f: S \u2192 S such that f(m\xB7n) = f(m)\xB7f(n), for every m, n \u2208 S and m\xB7n \u2208 S is equal to ______.",n:490,s:`Step 1: Put m = n = 1: f(1) = f(1)\xB2, so f(1) = 1 (0 is not in S).
Step 2: Products inside S (other than ones with 1) are only 2\xB72 = 4 and 2\xB73 = 6. So the rules are f(4) = f(2)\xB2 and f(6) = f(2)f(3).
Step 3: f(2)\xB2 must be in S, so f(2) is 1 or 2.
Step 4: If f(2) = 1: f(4) = 1, f(6) = f(3), any f(3) works: 7 choices. If f(2) = 2: f(4) = 4 and f(6) = 2f(3) \u2264 7, so f(3) \u2208 \uFF5B1, 2, 3\uFF5D: 3 choices. Pairs (f(2), f(3)): 7 + 3 = 10.
Step 5: f(5) and f(7) are free: 7 \xD7 7 = 49.
Step 6: Total = 10 \xD7 49 = 490 (also confirmed by brute force).
Answer: 490`},{pn:137,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"If a + \u03B1 = 1, b + \u03B2 = 2 and a\xB7f(x) + \u03B1\xB7f(1/x) = bx + \u03B2/x, x \u2260 0, then the value of the expression (f(x) + f(1/x))/(x + 1/x) is ______.",n:2,s:`Step 1: Given a\xB7f(x) + \u03B1\xB7f(1/x) = bx + \u03B2/x. (Equation 1)
Step 2: Replace x by 1/x: a\xB7f(1/x) + \u03B1\xB7f(x) = b/x + \u03B2x. (Equation 2)
Step 3: Add the two: (a + \u03B1)(f(x) + f(1/x)) = (b + \u03B2)(x + 1/x).
Step 4: Using a + \u03B1 = 1 and b + \u03B2 = 2: f(x) + f(1/x) = 2(x + 1/x).
Step 5: Divide by (x + 1/x): the expression equals 2.
Answer: 2`},{pn:138,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let the function f: [0, 1] \u2192 \u211D be defined by f(x) = 4^x/(4^x + 2). Then the value of f(1/40) + f(2/40) + f(3/40) + ... + f(39/40) - f(1/2) is ______.",n:19,s:`Step 1: Compute f(x) + f(1 - x). Let u = 4^x, so f(x) = u/(u + 2) and f(1 - x) = (4/u)/(4/u + 2) = 4/(4 + 2u) = 2/(2 + u).
Step 2: Sum = (u + 2)/(u + 2) = 1.
Step 3: In f(1/40) + ... + f(39/40), pair k/40 with (40 - k)/40: 19 pairs, each summing to 1, giving 19. The middle term is f(20/40) = f(1/2).
Step 4: So the sum = 19 + f(1/2). Subtracting f(1/2) leaves 19.
Answer: 19
Note: the exam year is read as 2010 from a blurred print (IIT-JEE ended in 2012).`},{pn:139,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:`Let the sets A and B denote the domain and range respectively of the function f(x) = 1/\u221A(\u2308x\u2309 - x), where \u2308x\u2309 denotes the smallest integer greater than or equal to x. Then among the statements
(S1): A \u2229 B = (1, \u221E) - \u2115 and
(S2): A \u222A B = (1, \u221E)`,o:["only (S1) is true","both (S1) and (S2) are true","only (S2) is true","neither (S1) nor (S2) is true"],c:0,s:`Step 1: \u2308x\u2309 - x is 0 when x is an integer and lies in (0, 1) otherwise. We need \u2308x\u2309 - x > 0 under the root, so x must not be an integer. Domain A = \u211D - \u2124.
Step 2: For non-integer x, t = \u2308x\u2309 - x takes every value in (0, 1). So \u221At takes every value in (0, 1) and f = 1/\u221At takes every value in (1, \u221E). Range B = (1, \u221E).
Step 3: A \u2229 B = (1, \u221E) without the integers = (1, \u221E) - \u2115 (the integers above 1 are removed, and 1 itself is not in B). So (S1) is true.
Step 4: A \u222A B contains all non-integers and also (1, \u221E), but the integers 0, -1, -2, ... and 1 are not in it, while (1, \u221E) alone misses the negative non-integers. So A \u222A B \u2260 (1, \u221E): (S2) is false.
Answer: (a) only (S1) is true`},{pn:140,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let f(x) = Ax\xB2 + Bx + C where A, B, C are real numbers. Prove that if f(x) is an integer whenever x is an integer, then the numbers 2A, A + B and C are all integers. Conversely, prove that if the numbers 2A, A + B and C are all integers, then f(x) is an integer whenever x is an integer.",a:"Proved: (forward) f(0) = C, f(1) - f(0) = A + B, and f(1) + f(-1) - 2f(0) = 2A are all integers; (converse) f(n) = (2A)\xB7n(n-1)/2 + (A + B)n + C is an integer.",s:`Step 1 (forward): Suppose f(n) is an integer for every integer n. Then f(0) = C is an integer.
Step 2: f(1) = A + B + C is an integer, and C is an integer, so A + B = f(1) - C is an integer.
Step 3: f(-1) = A - B + C is an integer. Add f(1) + f(-1) = 2A + 2C. Subtract 2C (an integer): 2A is an integer.
Step 4 (converse): Suppose 2A, A + B and C are integers. Rewrite Ax\xB2 + Bx = A(x\xB2 - x) + (A + B)x = (2A)\xB7x(x - 1)/2 + (A + B)x.
Step 5: For any integer n, n(n - 1) is a product of two consecutive integers, so it is even and n(n - 1)/2 is an integer.
Step 6: So f(n) = (2A)\xB7[n(n - 1)/2] + (A + B)\xB7n + C is a sum of products of integers, hence an integer.
Answer: Both parts are proved.`},{pn:141,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"dr",st:"Greatest integer and fractional part",q:"Let \uFF5Bx\uFF5D and [x] denote the fractional and integral part of a real number x respectively. Solve 4\uFF5Bx\uFF5D = x + [x].",a:"x = 0 or x = 5/3",s:`Step 1: Write x = [x] + \uFF5Bx\uFF5D. Let n = [x] (an integer).
Step 2: Then 4\uFF5Bx\uFF5D = x + [x] becomes 4\uFF5Bx\uFF5D = n + \uFF5Bx\uFF5D + n, so 3\uFF5Bx\uFF5D = 2n and \uFF5Bx\uFF5D = 2n/3.
Step 3: A fractional part satisfies 0 \u2264 \uFF5Bx\uFF5D < 1, so 0 \u2264 2n/3 < 1, i.e. 0 \u2264 n < 3/2. The integers possible are n = 0 and n = 1.
Step 4: n = 0 gives \uFF5Bx\uFF5D = 0, so x = 0 + 0 = 0. Check: 4\xB70 = 0 + 0. Correct.
Step 5: n = 1 gives \uFF5Bx\uFF5D = 2/3, so x = 1 + 2/3 = 5/3. Check: 4 \xD7 2/3 = 8/3 and x + [x] = 5/3 + 1 = 8/3. Correct.
Answer: x = 0 or x = 5/3`},{pn:142,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Find the natural number a for which \u03A3 (k = 1 to n) f(a + k) = 16(2\u207F - 1), where the function f satisfies the relation f(x + y) = f(x)f(y) for all natural numbers x, y and further f(1) = 2.",a:"a = 3",s:`Step 1: f(x + y) = f(x)f(y) with f(1) = 2 gives f(2) = f(1)\xB2 = 4, f(3) = 8, and in general f(n) = 2\u207F.
Step 2: So f(a + k) = 2^(a+k) = 2\u1D43\xB72\u1D4F.
Step 3: \u03A3 (k = 1 to n) f(a + k) = 2\u1D43(2 + 4 + ... + 2\u207F) = 2\u1D43\xB72(2\u207F - 1) = 2^(a+1)(2\u207F - 1) (geometric series).
Step 4: Equate with 16(2\u207F - 1): 2^(a+1) = 16 = 2\u2074.
Step 5: So a + 1 = 4, giving a = 3.
Answer: a = 3`},{pn:143,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Show that the value of tan x/tan 3x, wherever defined, never lies between 1/3 and 3.",a:"Proved: the value y satisfies y < 1/3 or y > 3.",s:`Step 1: Let t = tan x. Then tan 3x = (3t - t\xB3)/(1 - 3t\xB2), so y = tan x/tan 3x = t(1 - 3t\xB2)/(t(3 - t\xB2)) = (1 - 3t\xB2)/(3 - t\xB2). (t \u2260 0 since the ratio must be defined.)
Step 2: Let s = t\xB2 > 0. Then y(3 - s) = 1 - 3s, so 3y - ys = 1 - 3s, i.e. s(3 - y) = 1 - 3y.
Step 3: So s = (1 - 3y)/(3 - y) = (3y - 1)/(y - 3).
Step 4: Since s = tan\xB2x is real and positive, we need (3y - 1)/(y - 3) > 0.
Step 5: This is positive when both parts have the same sign: y > 3 (both positive) or y < 1/3 (both negative). For 1/3 \u2264 y \u2264 3 it is zero, negative or not defined, which is impossible.
Answer: y never lies between 1/3 and 3. Proved.`},{pn:144,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"func",st:"Functional equations",q:"Let \u211D be the set of real numbers and f: \u211D \u2192 \u211D be such that for all x and y in \u211D, |f(x) - f(y)| \u2264 |x - y|\xB3. Prove that f(x) is a constant.",a:"Proved: f'(x) = 0 for every x, so f is constant.",s:`Step 1: For x \u2260 y divide both sides by |x - y|: |f(x) - f(y)|/|x - y| \u2264 |x - y|\xB2.
Step 2: Fix x and let y \u2192 x. The right side |x - y|\xB2 tends to 0.
Step 3: So the left side, which is |(f(y) - f(x))/(y - x)|, also tends to 0 (it is squeezed between 0 and a quantity going to 0).
Step 4: That limit is |f'(x)|, so f'(x) = 0 for every real x.
Step 5: A function whose derivative is zero everywhere is constant.
Answer: f(x) is a constant. Proved.`},{pn:145,y:"IIT-JEE 1980",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Let y = \u221A((x + 1)(x - 3)/(x - 2)). Find all the real values of x for which y takes real values.",a:"x \u2208 [-1, 2) \u222A [3, \u221E)",s:`Step 1: y is real when the expression under the root is \u2265 0, with the denominator x - 2 \u2260 0.
Step 2: Critical points: x = -1, 2, 3 (zeros of numerator and denominator).
Step 3: Sign check of (x + 1)(x - 3)/(x - 2): for x > 3 it is positive; for 2 < x < 3 it is negative; for -1 < x < 2 it is positive; for x < -1 it is negative.
Step 4: Keep the positive parts. Include the zeros x = -1 and x = 3 (root of 0 is fine). Exclude x = 2 (division by zero).
Answer: x \u2208 [-1, 2) \u222A [3, \u221E)`},{pn:146,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"True or False: If f\u2081(x) and f\u2082(x) are defined on domains D\u2081 and D\u2082 respectively, then f\u2081(x) + f\u2082(x) is defined on D\u2081 \u2229 D\u2082.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`Step 1: To compute f\u2081(x) + f\u2082(x) at a point x, both f\u2081(x) and f\u2082(x) must exist.
Step 2: f\u2081(x) exists only for x \u2208 D\u2081 and f\u2082(x) only for x \u2208 D\u2082.
Step 3: So x must be in both, that is x \u2208 D\u2081 \u2229 D\u2082.
Answer: True`},{pn:147,y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"dr",st:"Even, odd and periodic functions",q:"Fill in the blank: If f is an even function defined on (-5, 5), then the four real values of x satisfying the equation f(x) = f((x + 1)/(x + 2)) are ______.",a:"(-1 + \u221A5)/2, (-1 - \u221A5)/2, (-3 + \u221A5)/2, (-3 - \u221A5)/2",s:`Step 1: f(x) = f(y) is certainly true if x = y, and, because f is even (f(-y) = f(y)), also if x = -y. We use both.
Step 2: Case x = (x + 1)/(x + 2): x\xB2 + 2x = x + 1, so x\xB2 + x - 1 = 0 and x = (-1 \xB1 \u221A5)/2.
Step 3: Case x = -(x + 1)/(x + 2): x\xB2 + 2x = -x - 1, so x\xB2 + 3x + 1 = 0 and x = (-3 \xB1 \u221A5)/2.
Step 4: Check the domain: all four values (about 0.618, -1.618, -0.382, -2.618) lie in (-5, 5), none equals -2, and (x + 1)/(x + 2) for each also lies in (-5, 5).
Answer: x = (-1 \xB1 \u221A5)/2 and x = (-3 \xB1 \u221A5)/2`},{pn:148,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"dr",st:"Domain and range",q:"Fill in the blank: If f(x) = sin(log(\u221A(4 - x\xB2)/(1 - x))), then the domain of f(x) is ______.",a:"(-2, 1)",s:`Step 1: sin is defined for all real inputs, so we only need log(...) to be defined: its argument \u221A(4 - x\xB2)/(1 - x) must be > 0.
Step 2: The square root needs 4 - x\xB2 \u2265 0, and since it is in a numerator with positive result required, 4 - x\xB2 > 0, so -2 < x < 2.
Step 3: \u221A(4 - x\xB2) is positive there, so the fraction is positive only if the denominator 1 - x > 0, i.e. x < 1.
Step 4: Combine -2 < x < 2 with x < 1: -2 < x < 1. (x = 1 is also excluded since the denominator would be 0.)
Answer: (-2, 1)`}]})();
